Apply to join: https://www.dataiq.global/2026-boston-peer-exchange/', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/dataiq-peer-exchange-boston-2026']::text[], now()),
  ('ai-ready-or-risky-a-working-session-for-c-level-and-equivalent-enterprise-leader@aiweek.boston', 'AI Ready or Risky? A working session for C-level and equivalent enterprise leaders actively pursuing an AI initiative.', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-ready-or-risky-a-working-session-for-c-level-and-equivalent-enterprise-leader', 'https://ai.northeastern.edu/ai-readiness-invitation#invitation-form', TRUE, '2026-10-01T08:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T12:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'EXP at Northeastern University', 'Northeastern University 8th Floor EXP 815 Columbus Avenue, 8th floor. Boston, MA 02120', 'Boston', 'Workshop', 'Northeastern University', '[{"name":"Usama Fayyad","bio":"Sr. VP for AI & Data Strategy at Northeastern University."},{"name":"Tom Davenport","bio":"President''s Distinguished Professor of Information Technology and Management at Babson College."},{"name":"Berkeley Almand-Hunter","bio":"Technical Director of Partner Products at Northeastern University''s Roux Institute."},{"name":"Dan Koloski","bio":"Head of Learning Programs and Professor of the Practice in the College of Professional Studies at Northeastern University''s Roux Institute."}]'::jsonb, 'AI Ready or Risky? A working session for C-level and equivalent enterprise leaders actively pursuing an AI initiative.', 'Your organization has identified an opportunity for AI. But is the business ready to move it forward?

AI Ready or Risky? is an invitation-only working session for C-level executives and equivalent enterprise leaders whose organizations are actively evaluating, piloting, implementing, or scaling an AI initiative. Unlike a traditional conference, this is a facilitated working experience. Each participant will arrive with one specific AI initiative and examine it across four areas that can determine whether it moves forward successfully: Business Value and ROI

Is the initiative connected to a meaningful business need? Are its expected value, costs, measures of success, and ownership clear?

People Readiness

Do employees, managers, and leaders have the skills, support, confidence, and ways of working required to adopt it?

Data Readiness

Is the necessary data available, usable, trustworthy, and appropriate for the proposed application?

Governance and Access

Can the organization manage access, oversight, accountability, security, and responsible use as the initiative advances?

How the working session works

Participants will move through the four readiness areas in small cohorts. At each station, experts will introduce key considerations, share practical examples, and guide participants in applying focused questions to the initiative they brought with them.

Throughout the session, attendees will capture their findings on a personalized AI Readiness Card, provided in both physical and digital formats. The completed card will provide a practical view of what is already in place, where gaps remain, and which questions or decisions must be addressed next.

You will leave with:

• A clearer view of your initiative''s readiness across four critical areas

• Greater visibility into the risks and unresolved questions that could impede progress

• Practical guidance from experts in AI strategy, workforce adoption, data, governance, and security

• A completed AI Readiness Card to support conversations with your leadership team

• A more focused set of priorities and next steps for advancing your initiative

Who should attend

This working session is designed for C-level executives and equivalent enterprise leaders who:

• Hold decision-making responsibility for an organizational AI initiative

• Are currently evaluating, piloting, implementing, or scaling AI

• Are prepared to bring one specific initiative to examine during the session

• Want a practical, cross-functional assessment rather than an introductory discussion about AI

Attendance is limited to preserve the small-group, interactive format. Invitation requests will be reviewed for alignment with the intended executive audience and working-session experience.

What You''ll Leave With

This is a working session, not a traditional panel discussion. You''ll leave with:

A completed AI Readiness Card for the specific initiative you brought to the event

A clearer view of where your initiative is ready to move forward and where risk or unanswered questions remain

Expert guidance across business value and ROI, people readiness, data readiness, and governance and access

A focused set of priorities and next steps to take back to your organization

New connections with fellow executives navigating similar AI decisions', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-ready-or-risky-a-working-session-for-c-level-and-equivalent-enterprise-leader']::text[], now()),
  ('agents-in-the-am@aiweek.boston', 'Agents in the AM', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/agents-in-the-am', 'https://page.datarobot.com/agents-in-the-am.html', TRUE, '2026-10-01T09:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T14:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'WeWork', '33 Arch St, Boston MA 02110', 'Boston', 'Workshop', 'DataRobot', '[{"name":"Amber Bennoui","bio":"Principal Product Manager (at DataRobot)."}]'::jsonb, 'Agents in the AM', 'Join us for Agents in the AM, an exclusive event where the hype ends and the building begins. Hear from experts, get hands-on in a practical lab, and walk out ready to run agents that actually scale.

For CIOs: We''ll guide you through a proven framework for moving beyond pilots and into a production-ready agent workforce that delivers measurable results.

For Developers: Roll up your sleeves and build. Learn the tools and approaches that make agents work in the real-world, not just in theory.

What you''ll experience:

CIOs sharing how they''re already running agents in production

Developer-tested tools and approaches for building scalable agents

A hands-on lab: build agents on DataRobot using NVIDIA', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/agents-in-the-am']::text[], now()),
  ('boston-ai-summit-2026@aiweek.boston', 'The Boston AI Summit 2026', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boston-ai-summit-2026', 'https://aiweek.boston/summit', FALSE, '2026-10-01T09:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T17:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Foundation Medicine', '400 Summer Street, Boston, MA 02210', 'Boston', 'Summit', 'Boston AI Week', '[{"name":"Judah Phillips","bio":"Founder & Board Chair of Boston AI Week."},{"name":"Chris Wright","bio":"Chief Technology Officer & Senior Vice President, Global Engineering at Red Hat."},{"name":"Meghan Barrett Welch","bio":"Chief Data Officer at the Massachusetts Executive Office of Energy & Environmental Affairs."},{"name":"Sabrina Mansur","bio":"Director of the Massachusetts Artificial Intelligence (AI) Hub."},{"name":"Emma Tippet","bio":"Head of Product at Starburst."},{"name":"John Huehn","bio":"Chief of Launchpad at Pegasystems Inc."},{"name":"Marco Montes de Oca","bio":"Principal AI Scientist at EnFi, Inc."},{"name":"Ken Sutton","bio":"Co-Founder & CEO of Yobe Inc."},{"name":"John Holmes","bio":"Managing Director at Accenture."},{"name":"Jenn Azar","bio":"CEO of Stellix."},{"name":"Murphy Vandervelde","bio":"Chief of Staff at Blitzy."},{"name":"Mike Grandinetti","bio":"Executive Fellow at Harvard Business School."},{"name":"Kleida Martiro","bio":"Managing Director at Glasswing Ventures."},{"name":"Patricia Geli","bio":"Co-Founder & Managing Partner at C10 Labs."},{"name":"David Chang","bio":"Co-Founder & CEO of Via AI."},{"name":"Mark Coffey","bio":"Co-Founder & Managing Partner at Misneach."},{"name":"Carol Meyers","bio":"Independent director, investor, and former GTM executive."},{"name":"Jonathan Corbin","bio":"Founder & CEO of Maven AGI."},{"name":"Scott Weller","bio":"Co-Founder & CTO of EnFi."},{"name":"Debbie Millin","bio":"Founder & CEO of UpperLevel Solutions."},{"name":"Dan Mitchell","bio":"Director of Startup Technology Ecosystem at Dell."},{"name":"Chris Menard","bio":"Chief Financial & Operating Officer at Gordon Brothers."}]'::jsonb, 'The Boston AI Summit 2026', 'The Boston AI Summit is the flagship conference of Boston AI Week: a single-track, one-room gathering of the executives, founders, investors, researchers, and public-sector leaders shaping AI in Massachusetts. The day opens with the official release of The State of AI in Massachusetts 2026, what people across the Commonwealth told us about how AI is actually being adopted, and builds from there: evidence, then discussion, then more evidence, until the closing conversation sends the week off.

How the day works

One room, one track: all 250 seats face the same stage. It opens with evidence: the State of AI report lands first, so every panel argues with the data already on the table. Research is the backbone: the day alternates research briefings and executive discussion. The schedule respects your day: doors at 9:00, program from 10:00 to 5:00, forty-minute panels, with breakfast, three breaks, and a long lunch built in.

Program

9:00 Registration, breakfast, and networking

10:00 Welcome: Massachusetts'' AI Advantage and the Work Ahead. Judah Phillips, Founder & Board Chair, Boston AI Week

10:10 Report release: The State of AI in Massachusetts 2026

10:25 Opening keynote (speaker to be announced)

11:20 Original research: Red Hat. Chris Wright, Chief Technology Officer & Senior Vice President, Global Engineering, Red Hat

12:00 Building AI: Meghan Barrett Welch (Chief Data Officer, Massachusetts Executive Office of Energy & Environmental Affairs), Sabrina Mansur (Director, Massachusetts Artificial Intelligence (AI) Hub), Emma Tippet (Head of Product, Starburst), John Huehn (Chief of Launchpad, Pegasystems Inc.)

12:40 Networking lunch

1:30 Original research: EnFi. Marco Montes de Oca, Principal AI Scientist, EnFi, Inc.

2:10 Managing AI: Ken Sutton (Co-Founder & CEO, Yobe Inc.), John Holmes (Managing Director, Accenture), Jenn Azar (CEO, Stellix), Murphy Vandervelde (Chief of Staff, Blitzy). Moderator: Mike Grandinetti, Executive Fellow, Harvard Business School

3:00 Investing in AI: Kleida Martiro (Managing Director, Glasswing Ventures), Patricia Geli (Co-Founder & Managing Partner, C10 Labs), David Chang (Co-Founder & CEO, Via AI), Mark Coffey (Co-Founder & Managing Partner, Misneach). Moderator: Carol Meyers, Independent Director, Investor, and Former GTM Executive

3:50 Entrepreneurs in AI: Jonathan Corbin (Founder & CEO, Maven AGI), Scott Weller (Co-Founder & CTO, EnFi), Debbie Millin (Founder & CEO, UpperLevel Solutions), Dan Mitchell (Director of Startup Technology Ecosystem, Dell). Additional panelists to be announced

4:30 Closing fireside, From Evidence to Execution: Judah Phillips in conversation with Chris Menard, Chief Financial & Operating Officer, Gordon Brothers

5:00 Adjourn. A networking reception follows.

Who''s in the room

The Summit is capped at 250 seats, and every application is reviewed to keep the room senior: executives, founders, investors, researchers, and public-sector leaders.

How to attend

Apply at aiweek.boston/summit (a Boston AI Week account is required). Applying takes two minutes, there is no cost to attend, and invitations go out in September on a rolling basis.

Getting there

Foundation Medicine at 400 Summer Street sits in the Seaport, a short walk from South Station''s Red Line and Commuter Rail service. Need an accommodation to participate? Email info@aiweek.boston.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boston-ai-summit-2026']::text[], now()),
  ('glasswing-massopen-agentic-101-2026@aiweek.boston', 'Glasswing and MassOpen Present: Agentic 101', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/glasswing-massopen-agentic-101-2026', 'https://luma.com/3kp1o3og', TRUE, '2026-10-01T09:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T18:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Glasswing Ventures', '275 Newbury St, Boston, MA 02116', 'Boston', 'Workshop', 'Glasswing Ventures and MassOpen.ai', '[{"name":"Jackie Peters","bio":"Co-founder of BlindInsight."},{"name":"Langdon White","bio":"Professor at Boston University."},{"name":"Jonathan Altman","bio":"Distinguished Engineer at GEICO."},{"name":"John Mark Walker","bio":"Open source product leader."}]'::jsonb, 'Glasswing and MassOpen Present: Agentic 101', 'Agentic 101 workshops are tailored for practitioners looking for practical ways to get started in open source agentic AI computing. How do you establish context for agents? How do you finetune models? How do you detect and correct drift? What is red-teaming in an agentic context? Attendees will come away with good ideas for getting started on their agentic journey.

For more information on MassOpen and the Agentic 101 workshop, see https://massopen.ai/agenda/oct01-bos/. Many thanks to our sponsors: The Open Accelerator, Red Hat and Stacklet.

Some of the presenters:

- Jackie Peters, co-founder, BlindInsight

- Langdon White, Professor at Boston University

- Jonathan Altman, Distinguished Engineer, GEICO

- John Mark Walker, open source product leader', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/glasswing-massopen-agentic-101-2026']::text[], now()),
  ('mit-futurefest-2026-day-2@aiweek.boston', 'MIT Future Fest — Day 2: Systems in Flux', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/mit-futurefest-2026-day-2', 'https://mitfuturefest.org/festival-passes', TRUE, '2026-10-01T10:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T21:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'MIT Campus', '77 Massachusetts Avenue, Cambridge, MA 02139', 'Cambridge', 'Community', 'MIT Future Fest (MIT Museum, MIT Technology Review & MIT Morningside Academy for Design)', '[]'::jsonb, 'MIT Future Fest — Day 2: Systems in Flux', 'Part of MIT Future Fest, the inaugural five-day festival where MIT opens its doors to the world — more than 50 events across campus spanning immersive exhibitions, open laboratories, public talks, and unexpected performances, presented by the MIT Museum, MIT Technology Review, and the MIT Morningside Academy for Design. One festival pass covers all five days. Session times vary; the full program launches August 17 at mitfuturefest.org/program.

The day starts in the body with sunrise movement and guided walks across campus, then expands into conversations on the systems that sustain our world — climate scientists, health researchers, and AI thinkers on what''s changing and what it takes to act. Future Labs open for hands-on research encounters, workshops dive into coastal resilience and ocean worldbuilding, and the evening turns to performance, salon, and speculative storytelling.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/mit-futurefest-2026-day-2']::text[], now()),
  ('safe-ai-governance-dont-let-ai-eat-your-lunch@aiweek.boston', 'Safe AI & Governance - “Don’t Let AI Eat Your Lunch”', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/safe-ai-governance-dont-let-ai-eat-your-lunch', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/safe-ai-governance-dont-let-ai-eat-your-lunch', FALSE, '2026-10-01T11:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T13:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Focus Technology Solutions – Conference Room / Mezzanine', '10 Post Office Square. Boston, MA 02109. It is on the 2nd floor "Floor M" can be accessed through the north elevator Office hours 7AM-5PM', 'Boston', 'Workshop', 'AI Advisor Guy + Focus Technology Solutions', '[{"name":"Kham Inthirath","bio":null},{"name":"Larry Veino","bio":null},{"name":"Craig Dedrick","bio":null}]'::jsonb, 'Safe AI & Governance - “Don’t Let AI Eat Your Lunch”', 'Your team is already using AI. The question is whether your business has the visibility, safeguards, and governance to use it safely.

Don''t Let AI Eat Your Lunch is a practical, interactive workshop for business leaders, executives, and IT leaders who want to adopt AI without exposing company data or creating risks they cannot control.

Led by AI Advisor Guy and Focus Technology Solutions, attendees will work through real-world scenarios covering AI access and permissions, shadow AI, workspace connections, human approval gates, deployment standards, and the questions every organization should ask its AI vendors.

Throughout the session, attendees will score their own organization using a Safe AI readiness framework. They will leave with their score, a practical audit checklist, and access to the full Safe AI Guide so they can identify gaps and take action immediately.

Lunch will be provided. The session includes interactive Q&A throughout.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/safe-ai-governance-dont-let-ai-eat-your-lunch']::text[], now()),
  ('boat-rides-boston-harbor-captain-chris-2026-10-01@aiweek.boston', 'Boat Rides of Boston Harbor with Captain Chris', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boat-rides-boston-harbor-captain-chris-2026-10-01', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boat-rides-boston-harbor-captain-chris-2026-10-01', FALSE, '2026-10-01T12:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T13:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Fan Pier', 'Fan Pier, Boston, MA', 'Boston', 'Meetup', 'Boston AI Week', '[]'::jsonb, 'Boat Rides of Boston Harbor with Captain Chris', 'Join Captain Chris for a boat ride on Boston Harbor, offered every day of Boston AI Week''s core week (September 24 – October 2). Very limited availability. RSVP to be considered, we''ll work with Captain Chris on approvals. Due to expected demand, not all RSVPs will be honored, but we''ll do our best.

Time: TBA. This is a request, not a confirmed spot — organizers will follow up if your RSVP is approved.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boat-rides-boston-harbor-captain-chris-2026-10-01']::text[], now()),
  ('ai-and-the-next-economy@aiweek.boston', 'AI and the Next Economy', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-and-the-next-economy', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-and-the-next-economy', FALSE, '2026-10-01T12:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T17:45:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Boston University, Duan Family Center for Computing & Data Sciences', '665 Commonwealth Avenue, Boston, MA 02215', 'Boston', 'Conference', 'Entrepreneur & Boston University Questrom School of Business', '[{"name":"Jason Feifer","bio":"Editor In Chief of Entrepreneur magazine; host of the event."},{"name":"Anand Mahurkar","bio":"Founder and CEO of Findability Sciences; keynote speaker on Implementation That Actually Works."},{"name":"Sam Goldberg","bio":"Co-Founder, Chief Strategy Officer and General Counsel at Unburdn.ai; featured in the live case and prompting breakout session."},{"name":"Paul Carlile","bio":"Peter and Deborah Wexler Professor of Information Systems and Senior Associate Dean for Research and Innovation at Boston University."},{"name":"H. Sami Karaca","bio":"Clinical Assistant Professor in Markets, Public Policy, & Law at Boston University."},{"name":"Venkat Venkatraman","bio":"Professor Emeritus in Information Systems at Boston University."},{"name":"Michel Anteby","bio":"Professor of Management & Organizations at Boston University."}]'::jsonb, 'AI and the Next Economy', 'Join Entrepreneur Events and Boston University’s Questrom School of Business for AI and the Next Economy during Boston’s AI Week on October 1, 2026.

Hosted by Entrepreneur magazine’s Editor In Chief Jason Feifer, this event brings together business leaders, entrepreneurs, faculty, students, alumni, and innovators for an applied exploration of how artificial intelligence is reshaping markets, enterprise strategy, organizational design, work, and human judgment. The program combines executive insight, live problem-solving, working breakout sessions, research translation, demonstrations, and relationship-building.

The summit is organized around a business-first, human-centered perspective. Each session connects AI to the decisions leaders make about value creation, operating models, workforce transformation, governance, and accountability.

Who should attend? Founders, AI Decision-Makers, CEOs, COOs, CTOs, Directors, Researchers

Keynote: Implementation That Actually Works

Anand Mahurkar, founder/CEO of Findability Sciences

Experimentation to Transformation: Framing the Challenge Live Case

Powered by the team at Unburdn.ai featuring Sam Goldberg, Co-Founder, Chief Strategy Officer and General Counsel at Unburdn.ai, and BU’s Paul Carlile, Peter and Deborah Wexler Professor of Information Systems and the Senior Associate Dean for Research and Innovation.

Breakout Sessions:

- Everything you learned about prompting is wrong with Unburdn.ai‘s Sam Goldberg & BU’s H. Sami Karaca, Clinical Assistant Professor in Markets, Public Policy, & Law

- From Pilot to P&L with Anand Mahurkar & BU’s Venkat Venkatraman, Professor Emeritus in Information Systems

- Leading an AI-Confident Workforce with BU’s Michel Anteby, Professor of Management & Organizations

Attendance is limited and by invitation. To request an invitation, please contact J.P. Matychak, matychak@bu.edu, while space remains.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-and-the-next-economy']::text[], now()),
  ('second-annual-ai-drug-discovery-symposium-revolutionizing-the-future-of-medicine@aiweek.boston', 'Second Annual AI & Drug Discovery Symposium:  Revolutionizing the Future of Medicine', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/second-annual-ai-drug-discovery-symposium-revolutionizing-the-future-of-medicine', 'https://www.goodwinlaw.com/en/news-and-events/events/2026/10/lifesciences-second-annual-ai-drug-discovery-symposium-revolutionizing-the-future-of-medicine', TRUE, '2026-10-01T13:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T18:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Goodwin', '100 Northern Avenue, Boston, Massachusetts, 02210', 'Boston', 'Conference', 'Goodwin', '[]'::jsonb, 'Second Annual AI & Drug Discovery Symposium:  Revolutionizing the Future of Medicine', 'Goodwin is hosting our Second Annual AI & Drug Discovery Symposium at our Boston office on October 1. This event will bring together leaders across life sciences, technology, and investment to discuss the opportunities and challenges shaping the next generation of AI-enabled drug discovery. Through engaging conversations and expert insights, we''ll explore emerging market and investment dynamics, innovative collaboration models, regulatory and policy considerations, and approaches to creating, protecting, and maximizing value across proprietary data, intellectual property, and AI-enabled platforms in an evolving life sciences landscape.

Discussion topics will include:

Market and Investment Trends in AI and Life Sciences

Redefining Collaboration: New Partnership Models in Drug Discovery

Regulatory & Policy Perspectives

Intellectual Property Strategy in the Age of AI

AI Governance and Security

The program will conclude with a networking reception, offering attendees the opportunity to connect with fellow executives, investors, advisors, and innovators across the life sciences and technology ecosystems over drinks and light refreshments.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/second-annual-ai-drug-discovery-symposium-revolutionizing-the-future-of-medicine']::text[], now()),
  ('architecture-and-ai-wentworth-2026@aiweek.boston', 'Architecture and AI', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/architecture-and-ai-wentworth-2026', 'https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScpv7dB7zMsvHfhfJIFq-Abvdlw2mNN77QTaZEMCYN56u1-sQ/viewform', TRUE, '2026-10-01T13:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T16:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Wentworth Institute of Technology', '550 Huntington Ave, Boston, MA 02115', 'Boston', 'Workshop', 'Wentworth Institute of Technology — School of Architecture and Design', '[{"name":"Antonio Furgiuele","bio":null},{"name":"Memo Ergezer","bio":null},{"name":"Tatjana Crossley","bio":null},{"name":"Ignacio Cardona","bio":null},{"name":"Katarina Richter-Lunn","bio":null},{"name":"George Guida","bio":null},{"name":"Danyson Tavares","bio":null},{"name":"Carla Gautier Castro","bio":null},{"name":"Caleb Hawkins","bio":null},{"name":"Jason Hasko","bio":null},{"name":"Angelica Facey","bio":null}]'::jsonb, 'Architecture and AI', 'Join the Wentworth Institute of Technology School of Architecture and Design (SoAD) for a special Boston AI Week event exploring how artificial intelligence is transforming architectural education, research, and practice.

This two-hour program brings together Wentworth faculty, researchers, alumni, and industry leaders for a series of rapid-fire PechaKucha-style presentations followed by a moderated roundtable discussion. Participants will examine emerging applications of AI in design, urbanism, fabrication, visualization, professional practice, and architectural pedagogy.

The first session features Wentworth faculty and researchers presenting current work in AI and design, including topics ranging from adversarial architecture and machine vision to urban analytics, digital representation, and computational design research.

The second session highlights Wentworth alumni and practitioners who are actively integrating AI into contemporary practice, offering perspectives from architecture, design technology, experiential media, professional organizations, and creative industries.

The event concludes with a moderated conversation and audience Q&A focused on the opportunities, challenges, and future implications of AI for architecture and design education and practice.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/architecture-and-ai-wentworth-2026']::text[], now()),
  ('datarobot-build-club-boston-ai-week-edition@aiweek.boston', 'DataRobot Build Club: Boston AI Week Edition', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/datarobot-build-club-boston-ai-week-edition', 'https://luma.com/7fe0z1nj', TRUE, '2026-10-01T14:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T16:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'WeWork Boston', '501 Boylston St, Boston, MA 02116', 'Boston', 'Meetup', 'DataRobot', '[]'::jsonb, 'DataRobot Build Club: Boston AI Week Edition', 'DataRobot Build Club is launching in Boston, and what better way to kick things off than during Boston AI Week!

Join us for an afternoon of conversations, bites and drinks with the developers and AI enthusiasts shaping the future of AI-powered development.

Meet fellow builders and swap ideas on what you''re working on. Show off a project, talk shop about agents and AI development, or just listen in and get to know Boston''s AI community.

DataRobot AI credits will be available for attendees, limited and first come first served.

Expect great conversations, demos from fellow builders, and the chance to connect with Boston''s AI community in a relaxed setting. No presentations, no pressure, just builders talking shop.

Whether you are experienced or completely new to DataRobot and agent development, you are welcome to join and be part of Build Club.

Limited capacity. RSVP required.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/datarobot-build-club-boston-ai-week-edition']::text[], now()),
  ('where-does-research-end-and-production-begin@aiweek.boston', 'Where Does Research End and Production Begin?', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/where-does-research-end-and-production-begin', 'https://partiful.com/e/j998GjHiZg2dSgGf4FSi', TRUE, '2026-10-01T16:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T17:15:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'MacPaw Cambridge office', '10 Canal Park 201, Cambridge, MA', 'Cambridge', 'Panel', 'MacPaw', '[{"name":"Mariya Hirna","bio":"AI & Research Manager at MacPaw."},{"name":"Maksym Kotliar","bio":"Staff Research Engineer at MacPaw."},{"name":"Alay Shah","bio":"Member of Technical Staff at Liquid AI."}]'::jsonb, 'Where Does Research End and Production Begin?', 'How do you build a product that needs technology that does not exist yet?

Join MacPaw AI&Research and Liquid AI for a conversation about what happens between a research result and a product someone actually uses.

We''ll get into the questions both teams run into every week:

- How do you run research so the results actually reach production?

- Where do you start when nobody has benchmarked what you need to measure?

- What tooling lets you absorb the next research result without rebuilding the system?

Examples will draw from three months of MacPaw and Liquid AI building an on-device AI stack for Apple Silicon together: Liquid Foundation Models, the Elix AI runtime, and the Mnemos memory layer.

Speakers:

- Mariya Hirna, AI & Research Manager at MacPaw

- Maksym Kotliar, Staff Research Engineer at MacPaw

- Alay Shah, Member of Technical Staff at Liquid AI

After the panel, stay for Maksym Kotliar''s talk on composable pipelines, then a mixer with snacks, coffee and soft drinks. A chance to keep the conversation going with the speakers and other builders in the room.

Agenda for the day:

4:00 – 4:30 PM: Doors open

4:30 – 5:15 PM: Panel — Where Does Research End and Production Begin?

5:30 – 6:15 PM: Talk — It''s 1952 Again: The Missing Compiler for AI Pipelines

6:15 – 8:30 PM: Mixer and networking', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/where-does-research-end-and-production-begin']::text[], now()),
  ('ai-hub-accelerator-alpfa-boston@aiweek.boston', 'AI Hub Accelerator - ALPFA Boston', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-hub-accelerator-alpfa-boston', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-hub-accelerator-alpfa-boston', FALSE, '2026-10-01T16:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T21:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Seaport Area', '101 Seaport Blvd, Boston, MA 02210', 'Boston', 'Workshop', 'ALPFA Boston', '[]'::jsonb, 'AI Hub Accelerator - ALPFA Boston', 'ALPFA Boston is excited to introduce the AI Hub Accelerator!

Four hands-on, practical workshops are happening simultaneously, designed to move you from AI awareness to real-world application.

Choose the workshop that best aligns with your goals and spend three focused hours learning and building with experts, including a 15-minute break:

- Workshop 1: Hands-On AI Coding Foundations — for entry-level professionals

- Workshop 2: Build Your Own AI Helpdesk — for small business owners

- Workshop 3: Agentic AI & Engineering Workflows — for STEM professionals

- Workshop 4: Enterprise AI Strategy — C-Suite Leaders (invite-only).

Join us alongside Resilient Coders, Roads Consulting Group US, MathWorks, and The Kendall Project, bringing practical expertise, industry perspectives, and real-world use cases into each learning experience.

Walk away with hands-on experience, practical skills, useful resources, and a work plan template to help you continue applying what you learned beyond the workshop.

End the night celebrating alongside fellow learners, industry partners, and AI innovators, and become an ALPFA Boston AI Hubber, part of a community built to keep learning, sharing, and growing together.

Choose your workshop. Bring your laptop. Build, connect, and become an ALPFA Boston AI Hubber.

Note: ALPFA Boston members can receive 50% or 100% off their event ticket.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-hub-accelerator-alpfa-boston']::text[], now()),
  ('boston-ai-week-venture-cafe@aiweek.boston', 'Boston AI Week @ Venture Café!', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boston-ai-week-venture-cafe', 'https://community.venturecafecambridge.org/events/1342?lang=en', TRUE, '2026-10-01T16:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T20:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Venture Café Cambridge, 5th Floor', 'CIC Cambridge, One Broadway, Cambridge, MA, 02142', 'Cambridge', 'Conference', 'Venture Café Cambridge, CIC', '[{"name":"Len Henry","bio":"Global Founder Advocate Program, Google Cloud for Startups. Topic: Agentic Security within Google Cloud."}]'::jsonb, 'Boston AI Week @ Venture Café!', 'Join the longtime startup tradition at Venture Café Cambridge for Boston AI Week: a celebration of innovation, ideas, and connection. Experience our Thursday Gathering at CIC, One Broadway, where innovators from diverse backgrounds come together to explore industry trends, share insights, and foster inclusive, equitable AI innovation. Attend expert-led sessions, engage with thought leaders, and network with a vibrant community that has grown to over 20,000 participants this year. Whether you’re a founder, professional, or curious thinker, Boston AI Week is your chance to connect, learn, and shape the future of technology in Boston.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boston-ai-week-venture-cafe']::text[], now()),
  ('agentforce-salesforce-2026@aiweek.boston', 'Harnessing AI through Agentforce in Salesforce', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/agentforce-salesforce-2026', 'https://trailblazercommunitygroups.com/events/details/salesforce-salesforce-marketer-group-boston-united-states-presents-unveiling-salesforce-agentforce-ai-insights-amp-demos-at-boston-ai-week-2026/', TRUE, '2026-10-01T17:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T19:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Kearney Boston Offices', '155 Seaport Blvd, 2nd floor, Boston, MA', 'Boston', 'Talk', 'Kearney', '[{"name":"Hunter Dunbar","bio":null},{"name":"Tim Ziter","bio":null},{"name":"Matt Brulet","bio":null},{"name":"Guilda Hilaire","bio":"Moderator."},{"name":"Michael Nelson","bio":null}]'::jsonb, 'Harnessing AI through Agentforce in Salesforce', 'Join us for a two hour session where we share the latest AI Agentforce innovations from Salesforce.

Speakers: Hunter Dunbar, Tim Ziter, Matt Brulet, Guilda Hilaire, Michael Nelson and many more.

Moderator: Guilda Hilaire.

Sponsors: Kearney, DataGroomr, Vonage.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/agentforce-salesforce-2026']::text[], now()),
  ('ai-strategy-to-enterprise-execution@aiweek.boston', 'From AI Strategy to Enterprise Execution', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-strategy-to-enterprise-execution', 'https://partiful.com/e/1UPm7YxDKBn659KydINs', TRUE, '2026-10-01T17:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T21:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Conference', 'TeamWork US', '[]'::jsonb, 'From AI Strategy to Enterprise Execution', 'Teamwork Corporate US is proud to host an executive event at Boston AI Week 2026, one of the most influential enterprise AI gatherings on the east coast. This invitation brings together CIOs, transformation leaders, and AI practitioners to explore how companies move beyond AI strategy into real, measurable business impact, through expert keynotes, roundtable discussions, and curated networking.

Senior leaders share what is actually working when moving AI from board-level strategy into production across the enterprise: operating model, governance, talent, vendor choices, and measurable outcomes. Venue and agenda confirmation TBA.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-strategy-to-enterprise-execution']::text[], now()),
  ('hacktoberfest-2026-hack-day-with-cloudinary-cambridge@aiweek.boston', 'Hacktoberfest 2026 Hack Day with Cloudinary [Cambridge]', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/hacktoberfest-2026-hack-day-with-cloudinary-cambridge', 'https://events.mlh.com/events/14720-hacktoberfest-2026-hack-day-with-cloudinary-cambridge', TRUE, '2026-10-01T17:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T20:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'The Foundry, Cambridge', '101 Rogers St', 'Cambridge', 'Workshop', 'Cloudinary', '[{"name":"Jen Looper","bio":null},{"name":"Ebony Louis","bio":null}]'::jsonb, 'Hacktoberfest 2026 Hack Day with Cloudinary [Cambridge]', 'Let''s gather for a community-focused media hackathon that combine hands-on building, technical mentorship, and developer networking. Join our interactive "make and showcase" session and collaborate in small groups or work individually to build media-rich applications leveraging Cloudinary for AI image/video transformations, optimized delivery, and automated visual workflows.

Event Structure & Agenda

Kickoff & Prompt Briefing: Welcome participants, introduce the theme/challenge (e.g., building with visual AI, media workflows, or web performance), and share API credentials, docs, and resources.

Brainstorming & Pitch Circle: Attendees share quick project ideas, ask questions, and form teams based on complementary skills or project goals.

Hacking & Mentorship Session: Participants spend dedicated time building their prototypes. Mentors and Cloudinary experts circulate to offer guidance on API integration, troubleshooting, and best practices.

Demos & Showcase: Each team delivers a 2-3 minute live demo highlighting their project, how they handled media optimization or transformations, and what they built.

Wrap-Up & Networking: Celebrate top projects, award Hacktoberfest and Cloudinary swag, and open the floor for informal networking and discussion.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/hacktoberfest-2026-hack-day-with-cloudinary-cambridge']::text[], now()),
  ('its-1952-again-the-missing-compiler-for-ai-pipelines-talkmeet-up-after@aiweek.boston', 'It''s 1952 Again: The Missing Compiler for AI Pipelines (talk+meet-up after)', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/its-1952-again-the-missing-compiler-for-ai-pipelines-talkmeet-up-after', 'https://partiful.com/e/opyw9DpUqapGSRBcSHnH', TRUE, '2026-10-01T17:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T19:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'MacPaw HQ', '10 Canal Park 201, Cambridge, MA', 'Cambridge', 'Talk', 'MacPaw', '[{"name":"Maksym Kotliar","bio":"Staff Research Engineer at MacPaw; talk on composable AI pipelines."}]'::jsonb, 'It''s 1952 Again: The Missing Compiler for AI Pipelines (talk+meet-up after)', 'It''s 1952 Again: The Missing Compiler for AI Pipelines

Composable AI Pipelines by Maksym Kotliar

AI orchestration in 2026 looks a lot like programming in 1952: hand-tuned prompts, model-specific quirks, and all the bookkeeping left to the developer. Programming solved this problem once, with the compiler. Maksym Kotliar will walk through why the same fix applies to AI pipelines, and how MacPaw built one.

In this talk:

- Why swapping AI models still means rewriting your orchestration code, and what that has in common with programming before FORTRAN

- Composable Pipelines, a Swift DSL where you declare what a step should do and let the runtime decide how

- How a single macOS process can serve every AI app on the machine, loading local models once and sharing the GPU

Open source, Apache 2.0: github.com/MacPaw/ComposablePipelines

After the talk, stay for an informal meetup over snacks, coffee and soft drinks. A chance to keep talking about on-device AI with the speakers and the other builders in the room.

Agenda:

5:30 – 6:15 PM: Talk on Composable pipelines + audience Q&A

6:15 – 8:00 PM: meetup & networking', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/its-1952-again-the-missing-compiler-for-ai-pipelines-talkmeet-up-after']::text[], now()),
  ('building-ai-agents-for-founders-and-fractional-leaders@aiweek.boston', 'Building AI Agents for Founders and Fractional Leaders', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/building-ai-agents-for-founders-and-fractional-leaders', 'https://luma.com/2aqbxxsz', TRUE, '2026-10-01T17:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T19:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'MIT', 'Boston Common 1P, Charles St, Boston, MA 02116, United States', 'Boston', 'Workshop', 'Founder of Theona and Egbe, Managing Partner at Agentic Builders, and founder & CEO of Hyperskill', '[{"name":"Nikolay Vyahhi","bio":"Founder of Theona and Egbe, Managing Partner at Agentic Builders, and founder & CEO of Hyperskill."}]'::jsonb, 'Building AI Agents for Founders and Fractional Leaders', 'Walk in with the tools you already use. Walk out with a working operational system built from your own processes.

A two-hour working session. Connect a tool you already use, map your processes with the Space Architect, and set up the agents that run them.

Format

In-person, hands-on. Hosted by Nikolay Vyahhi.

Who this is for

Founders of small operating businesses where most of the work still routes through them. Fractional COOs, fractional sales, and fractional ops professionals working across multiple clients. Ops leads at small and mid-size companies. Sales professionals managing high volume.

The format assumes you already have processes that exist in some form. Theona is built to map and automate work that already happens, not to design operations from scratch.

What happens during the session

Each participant logs and connects one service where most of their work lives. Examples include Notion, Slack, HubSpot, Linear, Gmail, or meeting recordings. One tool is enough to give the Architect context.

You will create a Space, named after a business function or a specific client, and describe their process to the Architect in their own words.

What you leave with

A Space containing your processes. Agent stubs across the workflow. One agent set up and running. For fractional roles, the agents built during the session can later be handed to clients as part of a delivered operational layer.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/building-ai-agents-for-founders-and-fractional-leaders']::text[], now()),
  ('aws-startups-x-boston-ai-week-ai-visionaries-dinner@aiweek.boston', 'AWS Startups x Boston AI Week: AI Visionaries Dinner', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/aws-startups-x-boston-ai-week-ai-visionaries-dinner', 'https://www.capriboston.com/', TRUE, '2026-10-01T17:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T21:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Capri Boston', '500 Harrison Ave, Boston, MA 02118', 'Boston', 'Meetup', 'Amazon Web Services', '[]'::jsonb, 'AWS Startups x Boston AI Week: AI Visionaries Dinner', 'Private event — guest list selected by AWS.

This event brings together CEO and CTO-level leaders from pioneering startups for an exclusive evening of peer-driven conversation and connection during Boston AI Week. Hosted at Capri in Boston, this intimate gathering begins with cocktails and networking, followed by moderated roundtable discussions over a multi-course dinner. This elegant setting offers a unique opportunity to exchange insights on scaling AI companies, share lessons from the trenches, and explore potential collaborations with fellow builders shaping the future of the ecosystem.

This evening is tailored for founders and C-suite leaders from AI and tech startups who are driving innovation in generative AI, agentic systems, and related technologies.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/aws-startups-x-boston-ai-week-ai-visionaries-dinner']::text[], now()),
  ('quay-acceleration-x-hub-tokyo-boston-demo-day-2026@aiweek.boston', 'QUAY Acceleration × X-Hub Tokyo-Boston 2026 Demo Day, in Partnership with JETRO & Tokyo Metropolitan Government', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/quay-acceleration-x-hub-tokyo-boston-demo-day-2026', 'https://luma.com/g8j4zu5a', TRUE, '2026-10-01T17:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T20:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Boston, MA — exact venue shared upon registration', NULL, 'Boston', 'Demo Night', 'QUAY Acceleration', '[{"name":"CREATANT","bio":"Fall 2026 cohort startup."},{"name":"RoboFounders","bio":"Fall 2026 cohort startup."},{"name":"You Need Inc.","bio":"Fall 2026 cohort startup."},{"name":"GlobalDeal","bio":"Fall 2026 cohort startup."},{"name":"Tokoshié","bio":"Fall 2026 cohort startup."},{"name":"LobbyAI","bio":"Fall 2026 cohort startup."},{"name":"Neuspective","bio":"Fall 2026 cohort startup."},{"name":"New Space Intelligence","bio":"Fall 2026 cohort startup."},{"name":"BagnaCauda","bio":"Fall 2026 cohort startup."},{"name":"COGNITEE","bio":"Fall 2026 cohort startup."}]'::jsonb, 'QUAY Acceleration × X-Hub Tokyo-Boston 2026 Demo Day, in Partnership with JETRO & Tokyo Metropolitan Government', 'Join us in Boston for the QUAY Acceleration × X-Hub Tokyo-Boston 2026 Demo Day, in partnership with JETRO and the Tokyo Metropolitan Government.

QUAY designs and runs accelerator programs for governments and economic development organizations globally — 900+ startups accelerated across 40+ countries, with $1.1B+ raised by portfolio companies post-program. The X-Hub Tokyo-Boston program begins with a bootcamp in Tokyo, continues virtually, and concludes with an immersive week in Boston during Boston AI Week, culminating in this Demo Day.

The Fall 2026 cohort is ten high-growth, AI-driven startups from Japan — spanning data tech, robotics, industrial tech, health tech, fintech, commerce, climate tech, and media — joined by representatives from JETRO and the Tokyo Metropolitan Government: CREATANT, RoboFounders, You Need Inc., GlobalDeal, Tokoshié, LobbyAI, Neuspective, New Space Intelligence, BagnaCauda, and COGNITEE.

Founders showcase their work and pitch their companies, and the room brings together the Boston investors, operators, and partners who can pull them forward.

Space is limited and registration is subject to host approval.

Questions: hello@quay.co', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/quay-acceleration-x-hub-tokyo-boston-demo-day-2026']::text[], now()),
  ('women-in-data-fall-happy-hour@aiweek.boston', 'Women in Data - Fall Happy Hour', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/women-in-data-fall-happy-hour', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/women-in-data-fall-happy-hour', FALSE, '2026-10-01T18:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T20:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Boston Harbor Distillery at the Greenway', '369 Atlantic Avenue Boston, MA 02110', 'Boston', 'Meetup', 'Women in Data', '[]'::jsonb, 'Women in Data - Fall Happy Hour', 'The Boston Women In Data™ chapter and Pyladies Boston chapter are co-hosting our Fall Happy Hour on Thursday, Oct 1st at the Boston Harbor Distillery At The Greenway! Whether you’re a data professional or looking to learn more about the space, come connect and meet like-minded people!', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/women-in-data-fall-happy-hour']::text[], now()),
  ('your-ai-chatbot-is-live-now-what@aiweek.boston', 'Make AI Work: Enterprise AI that companies actually buy, use, and keep', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/your-ai-chatbot-is-live-now-what', 'https://www.eventbrite.com/e/make-ai-work-enterprise-ai-that-companies-actually-buy-use-and-keep-tickets-1999807173088?aff=oddtdtcreator', TRUE, '2026-10-01T18:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T20:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '661 Mass Ave, 3rd Floor', '661 Mass Ave, 3rd FL, Arlington, MA 02476', 'Arlington', 'Panel', 'Weihs Systems Consultants', '[{"name":"Harry Glorikian","bio":"Healthcare entrepreneur, investor, and author; brings the investor and commercialization perspective on where AI creates durable business value."},{"name":"Ben Shiller","bio":"Economist and Professor at Brandeis University; researches the economics of technology, AI, productivity, and innovation."}]'::jsonb, 'Make AI Work: Enterprise AI that companies actually buy, use, and keep', 'Getting an AI application into production is no longer the finish line. The harder question is whether it creates value.

This panel brings together perspectives from AI product development, economics, and investment. What happens after the prototype works and real users arrive? What separates successful production AI from an expensive experiment? The panelists have shipped and analyzed AI products and services in diverse industries.

We''ll examine the realities of making AI successful in production: choosing the right problems, driving adoption, redesigning workflows, measuring business impact, and dealing with the reliability, evaluation, privacy, guardrails, and human oversight that emerge when AI moves from demo to daily use.

The conversation will move beyond "Can we build it?" to the question that increasingly matters: Is the AI making the business better?

Meet practitioners and network before and after the panel, light refreshments.

About our panelists

Harry Glorikian is a healthcare entrepreneur, investor, and author who has spent decades at the intersection of technology, data, and life sciences. He brings the investor and commercialization perspective on where AI creates durable business value — and where the math doesn''t hold up.

Ben Shiller is an economist and professor at Brandeis University whose research examines the economics of technology, AI, productivity, and innovation. He brings an economist''s lens to the question of how we determine whether AI is creating measurable value.

A third panelist will be announced.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/your-ai-chatbot-is-live-now-what']::text[], now()),
  ('ai-storytelling-and-organizational-change-a-dinner-with-gamma@aiweek.boston', 'AI, Storytelling, and Organizational Change: A Dinner with Gamma', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-storytelling-and-organizational-change-a-dinner-with-gamma', 'https://luma.com/meetgamma-a0fa', TRUE, '2026-10-01T19:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-01T21:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'To be announced.', 'Boston/ Cambridge', 'Boston', 'Meetup', 'Gamma', '[]'::jsonb, 'AI, Storytelling, and Organizational Change: A Dinner with Gamma', 'Join us for an intimate dinner with Gamma exploring how AI is reshaping the way organizations communicate, make decisions, and bring people along through change. Over a relaxed evening with a small group of leaders, we''ll trade honest stories about what''s actually working — and what storytelling looks like when the tools, and the pace, have fundamentally shifted.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-storytelling-and-organizational-change-a-dinner-with-gamma']::text[], now()),
  ('tiecon-east-2026-the-next-growth-playbook-product-market-capital-in-the-age-of-a@aiweek.boston', 'TiECON East 2026 THE NEXT GROWTH PLAYBOOK: Product, Market & Capital in the Age of AI', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/tiecon-east-2026-the-next-growth-playbook-product-market-capital-in-the-age-of-a', 'https://events.tie.org/Boston/TiECONEast2026', TRUE, '2026-10-02T07:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-02T19:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Burlington Marriott Hotel', 'One Burlington Mall Road, Burlington, MA 01803', 'Burlington', 'Conference', 'TiE Boston', '[{"name":"Jeff Elton","bio":"Vice Chair and Founding CEO of ConcertAI."},{"name":"David Friend","bio":"President and CEO of Wasabi Technologies."},{"name":"Zenobia Moochhala","bio":"Undersecretary of Business Strategies for the Commonwealth of Massachusetts."}]'::jsonb, 'TiECON East 2026 THE NEXT GROWTH PLAYBOOK: Product, Market & Capital in the Age of AI', 'TiECON East 2026, co-hosted by TiE Boston and TiE New York, will take place on Friday, October 2, 2026, at the Burlington Marriott. This year’s theme, “The Next Growth Playbook: Product, Market & Capital in the Age of AI,” will bring together founders, investors, business leaders, and technology executives to explore how AI is reshaping the way companies build products, reach markets, raise capital, and scale. Bringing together the strength and networks of two leading East Coast innovation ecosystems, TiECON East provides a dynamic platform for thought leadership, meaningful connections, investment, and business growth.

This event is independently created and produced by its host and is featured by Boston AI Week as part of The Festival of AI.

Ready to join us? Register today and save on your ticket.

Use promo code AIWEEK at checkout to receive a discount on General Admission.

Register now → Enter AIWEEK at checkout → Save on admission', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/tiecon-east-2026-the-next-growth-playbook-product-market-capital-in-the-age-of-a']::text[], now()),
  ('ai-education-and-the-future-of-how-we-prove-what-we-know@aiweek.boston', 'AI, Education, and the Future of How We Prove What We Know', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-education-and-the-future-of-how-we-prove-what-we-know', 'https://trusted.bu.edu/s/1759/2-bu/19/1col.aspx?sid=1759&gid=2&pgid=19867&content_id=20993', TRUE, '2026-10-02T08:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-02T10:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'WBUR City Space', '890 Commonwealth Ave, Boston, MA 02215', 'Boston', 'Talk', 'Questrom School of Business Ravi K. Mehrotra Institute, and Feld Center for Career and Alumni Engagement', '[{"name":"Maria Flynn","bio":"President and CEO of Jobs for the Future."},{"name":"Janet Kang","bio":"Executive Director of Just Horizons Alliance."},{"name":"Chris Dellarocas","bio":"Richard C. Shipley Professor of Information Systems at Boston University."},{"name":"Curt Nickisch","bio":"Host."}]'::jsonb, 'AI, Education, and the Future of How We Prove What We Know', 'Join us for a live taping of “Is Business Broken?”, the Boston University Questrom School of Business Ravi K. Mehrotra Institute’s podcast in partnership with WBUR, featuring a candid conversation with leading voices on the future of AI.

Co-hosted with the Questrom School’s Feld Center for Career and Alumni Engagement as part of Boston AI Week, this timely discussion will examine how AI is reshaping learning, credentials, and work. From the classroom to the labor market, our panelists will explore who stands to benefit, who may bear the risk, and what the long-term implications may be for education, business, and society in the age of AI.

The event will begin with breakfast and a fantastic opportunity to network with faculty, researchers, industry, and students.

Pre-registration is required to attend.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-education-and-the-future-of-how-we-prove-what-we-know']::text[], now()),
  ('2nd-annual-ai-powered-innovation-in-protein-and-antibody-therapeutics-symposium@aiweek.boston', '2nd Annual AI-Powered Innovation in Protein and Antibody Therapeutics Symposium', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/2nd-annual-ai-powered-innovation-in-protein-and-antibody-therapeutics-symposium', 'https://www.biolinkevents.com/event-details/ai-powered-innovation-in-protein-and-antibody-therapeutics', TRUE, '2026-10-02T08:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-02T17:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Le Meridien Cambridge', '20 Sidney St, Cambridge, MA 02139', 'Cambridge', 'Conference', 'Sino Biological', '[{"name":"Dr. Diane Joseph-McCarthy","bio":"Executive Director, Bioengineering Technology & Entrepreneurship Center; Professor of the Practice, Biomedical Engineering & Chemistry, Boston University; AIMBE fellow (Opening Keynote)."},{"name":"Dr. Abigail Lytton-Jean","bio":"Founding Scientific Director, NanoFIB, Koch Institute for Integrative Cancer Research at MIT (Scientific Keynote)."},{"name":"Dr. Matthew Bakalar","bio":"Head of Innovation Science, Basecamp Research (Industry Keynote)."},{"name":"Dr. Daniel Bedinger","bio":"Senior Manager, Field Application Science, Carterra."},{"name":"Dr. Leo Wan","bio":"Founder and CEO, Ranomics."},{"name":"Dr. Will Cao","bio":"Co-Founder & CEO, Pando Bioscience."},{"name":"Devan Shah","bio":"Founder & CEO, RNAV8 Bio."},{"name":"Allison Colthart","bio":"Sr Principal Scientist, Seismic Therapeutic."}]'::jsonb, '2nd Annual AI-Powered Innovation in Protein and Antibody Therapeutics Symposium', 'Sino Biological invites you to the 2nd Annual AI-Powered Innovation in Protein and Antibody Therapeutics (AIPAT) Symposium, a premier gathering of leading minds dedicated to shaping the future of biopharma discovery.

This year’s symposium features deep dives into machine learning models, in silico screening, and bridging the crucial gap between computationally designed proteins and real-world clinical application.

Join 120 industry leaders and researchers for a day of networking and innovation at Le Méridien Cambridge on October 2nd to connect with industry pioneers, spark new collaborations, and help drive the next generation of AI-driven therapeutics. Our program features 7 great speakers from both industry and academia, ample networking opportunities, 8 oral presenters competing for over $5,000 in prizes, exhibitors, and happy hour reception!

Submit abstracts on our website until August 31st!', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/2nd-annual-ai-powered-innovation-in-protein-and-antibody-therapeutics-symposium']::text[], now()),
  ('workforce-innovation-ai-driven-technology@aiweek.boston', 'Workforce Innovation & AI-Driven Technology', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/workforce-innovation-ai-driven-technology', 'https://www.eventbrite.com/e/workforce-innovation-ai-driven-technology-tickets-1999041143874?aff=oddtdtcreator', TRUE, '2026-10-02T08:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-02T16:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'The Westin Waltham Boston', '70 3rd Ave, Waltham, MA 02451', 'Boston', 'Conference', 'MassAbility', '[]'::jsonb, 'Workforce Innovation & AI-Driven Technology', 'MassAbility invites you to join AI and technology leaders, businesses, workforce experts, and startup innovators for an interactive day exploring how AI, emerging technologies, and human ingenuity are transforming the way we work. Discover new approaches to finding talent, building skills, redesigning jobs, and creating agile, innovative, future-ready teams. Through interactive conversations, demonstrations, and real-world examples, we’ll explore how AI and innovation can strengthen talent pipelines, expand workforce participation, and unlock new opportunities—including for people with disabilities. Come ready to explore, experiment, connect, and imagine what’s possible.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/workforce-innovation-ai-driven-technology']::text[], now()),
  ('builders-and-brews@aiweek.boston', 'Builders and Brews', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/builders-and-brews', 'https://luma.com/12snuaq9', TRUE, '2026-10-02T09:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-02T17:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Cambridge', NULL, 'Cambridge', 'Workshop', 'The AI Runtime', '[]'::jsonb, 'Builders and Brews', 'A day of agents, coffee, and good company

Pull up for a day with Tavily, Microsoft, and Nebius.

We’re hosting a laid-back builder day in Boston focused on real-world, web-grounded agents, no pressure, no pitches, just building, experimenting, and meeting great people.

Whether you’re deep in agent workflows or just getting started, this is a space to explore, connect, and create.

What’s happening

Tavily is the real-time search engine for AI agents and RAG workflows, fast, secure APIs for web search and content extraction.

Nebius provides scalable AI cloud infrastructure, helping teams train, deploy, and scale AI applications efficiently.

Together, we’re bringing builders, founders, and engineers into one space for an evening of hands-on building and conversation.

Come curious. Leave caffeinated.

What you get

☕ Free coffee (or de-caffeinated drinks)

⚡ Tavily Credits (ttps://www.tavily.com/)

⚡ Nebius Token Factory credits

🛠️ Presentations: How to build with Tavily & Nebius

🧱 Time and space to build (or improve what you’re already working on)

💬 Direct access to the Tavily & Nebius

Who should come

Builders curious about agent tooling

Founders & engineers working with real-time web data

Anyone exploring AI agents

Investors & operators

Coffee lovers (important)

Music Lovers

How it works

Drop in anytime during the event

Co-work, network, enjoy good Music or just hang out

Build something (or don’t - conversations are just as valuable)

📍 Location: Boston (shared after RSVP)

🕕 Time: 9:00 AM – 5:00 PM PT

🎟 Cost: Free', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/builders-and-brews']::text[], now()),
  ('aws-ai-league-drug-discovery-challenge@aiweek.boston', 'AWS AI League: Drug Discovery Challenge', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/aws-ai-league-drug-discovery-challenge', 'https://awsaileaguedrugdiscoveryqachal.splashthat.com/', TRUE, '2026-10-02T09:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-02T13:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'AWS Office BOS17 Level 1, Room 1.101', '27 Melcher St, Boston, MA 02210, Boston, MA 02210, 27 Melcher St, Boston, MA 02210', 'Boston', 'Workshop', 'AWS', '[]'::jsonb, 'AWS AI League: Drug Discovery Challenge', 'Overview

Fine-tune a Small Language Model (SLM) like Amazon Nova Micro (via Amazon SageMaker AI) for drug discovery — identify therapeutic targets and predict molecular properties with higher accuracy than what''s possible with frontier-scale models, and at lower costs.

Your fine-tuned SLM can either integrate multi-modal data to discover disease-specific targets, or predict molecular properties like permeability given a molecule''s SMILES string — the same categories that determine whether a drug candidate survives to the clinic.

This follows the same approach used by Insilico Medicine (TargetPro) and Nimbus Therapeutics (Novus model) on AWS: supervised fine-tuning on thousands of labeled multi-modal data, then iterating on prompts, data mixing, and training strategy to push accuracy higher.

You can team up with others to prepare training data, configure fine-tuning jobs on SageMaker AI, and submit predictions to an automated evaluation pipeline that scores against experimental measurements in real time. Sample datasets like TargetBench and ChEMBL in JSONL Q&A pairs will be provided, and you can bring your own datasets as well. The team with the most accurate predictions wins up to $5K in AWS credits.

Agenda

9:00 AM – 9:30 AM | Breakfast & Networking — Grab coffee and breakfast, meet fellow builders, founders, and Amazonians. Setup AWS Workshop Studio environment plus Amazon Quick Desktop, and a walkthrough of the day''s format.

9:30 AM – 11:30 AM | Build Starts — Hands-on build sprint: use Amazon Quick to prepare datasets for model fine tuning; take advantage of serverless model fine tuning capability on Amazon SageMaker AI to develop your customized model. Deploy your best model to Amazon Bedrock. Use Amazon Quick Desktop as your scientific workbench to interact with your own model for competitive intelligent analysis.

11:30 AM – 12:00 PM | Leaderboard Showcase — Submit your customized model on pre-built leaderboard — we will evaluate the model capability against certain tasks. Winners are rewarded with AWS credits and invited to showcase your models to AWS leadership.

What You''ll Walk Away With

- A fine-tuned model you built, hands-on experience with Amazon SageMaker AI, and potential prizes up to $5K in AWS credits.

Prizes

Category: Target Identification — 1st Place: $3,000 in AWS Promotional Credits + AWS AI League Championship invitation; 2nd Place: $2,000 in AWS Promotional Credits; 3rd Place: $500 in AWS Promotional Credits.

Category: Molecular Property Prediction — 1st Place: $3,000 in AWS Promotional Credits + AWS AI League Championship invitation; 2nd Place: $2,000 in AWS Promotional Credits; 3rd Place: $500 in AWS Promotional Credits.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/aws-ai-league-drug-discovery-challenge']::text[], now()),
  ('mit-futurefest-2026-day-3@aiweek.boston', 'MIT Future Fest — Day 3: Frontiers in Focus', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/mit-futurefest-2026-day-3', 'https://mitfuturefest.org/festival-passes', TRUE, '2026-10-02T10:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-02T21:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'MIT Campus', '77 Massachusetts Avenue, Cambridge, MA 02139', 'Cambridge', 'Community', 'MIT Future Fest (MIT Museum, MIT Technology Review & MIT Morningside Academy for Design)', '[]'::jsonb, 'MIT Future Fest — Day 3: Frontiers in Focus', 'Part of MIT Future Fest, the inaugural five-day festival where MIT opens its doors to the world — more than 50 events across campus spanning immersive exhibitions, open laboratories, public talks, and unexpected performances, presented by the MIT Museum, MIT Technology Review, and the MIT Morningside Academy for Design. One festival pass covers all five days. Session times vary; the full program launches August 17 at mitfuturefest.org/program.

A day at the frontiers of the possible: quantum science, AI research, and the future of making. Future Labs run across campus with rare access to materials research, hands-on experimentation runs alongside big-picture conversations on artificial intelligence and governance, and the evening builds to a science salon and a performance weaving cinema, music, and Afrofuturism.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/mit-futurefest-2026-day-3']::text[], now()),
  ('grade-the-work-can-your-managers-tell-good-ai-output-from-bad-a-live-test@aiweek.boston', 'Grade the Work: Would Your Managers Trust the Same AI-Assisted Work?', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/grade-the-work-can-your-managers-tell-good-ai-output-from-bad-a-live-test', 'https://www.eventbrite.com/e/grade-the-work-would-your-managers-trust-the-same-ai-assisted-work-tickets-1997855016134', TRUE, '2026-10-02T10:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-02T11:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Online', NULL, 'Virtual', 'Workshop', 'Work Redesigned', '[]'::jsonb, 'Grade the Work: Would Your Managers Trust the Same AI-Assisted Work?', 'AI can turn rough notes into polished work in seconds. The result may look finished long before anyone has established whether it deserves to be trusted.

For HR and People Leaders setting quality expectations across the whole organization, this creates a real challenge. Weak work moves forward, sound work gets redone, and HR cannot show leadership that human review is working consistently.

In this live online working session, participants will evaluate three realistic examples of AI-assisted work. We will share anonymous results on screen and see where judgments align and where they differ.

Next, participants will assess the same work again using a one-page standard covering evidence, reasoning, fit for purpose, human accountability, and the consequences of being wrong. Together, we will compare the changes and explore how we can improve this.

Participants will leave with a practical starting standard and a self-assessment for finding where their organization relies on individual judgement instead of a clear, consistent standard.

No technical background is needed.

This workshop is presented in partnership with ATD Greater Boston and Work Redesigned.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/grade-the-work-can-your-managers-tell-good-ai-output-from-bad-a-live-test']::text[], now()),
  ('ai-meets-clinical-data-what-actually-breaks-and-what-automates@aiweek.boston', 'AI Meets Clinical Data: What Actually Breaks, and What Automates', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-meets-clinical-data-what-actually-breaks-and-what-automates', 'https://lu.ma/9qojmptf', TRUE, '2026-10-02T12:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-02T13:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Talk', 'CDM-App (clinical data workflow automation)', '[{"name":"Featured clinical data manager","bio":null},{"name":"Marius Pasca","bio":"Regulated-data-systems engineer at CDM-App who hosts this session, comparing how high-stakes data gets reconciled and trusted across clinical data management and regulated industries like banking and trading."}]'::jsonb, 'AI Meets Clinical Data: What Actually Breaks, and What Automates', 'A candid fireside on where AI genuinely helps clinical data management — and where it doesn''t. A practicing clinical data manager and an engineer from regulated-data systems (banking, trading) compare how high-stakes data actually gets reconciled, cleaned, and trusted across industries — and what clinical teams should, and shouldn''t, hand to automation. Practitioner-led, technical, honest over hype.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-meets-clinical-data-what-actually-breaks-and-what-automates']::text[], now()),
  ('boat-rides-boston-harbor-captain-chris-2026-10-02@aiweek.boston', 'Boat Rides of Boston Harbor with Captain Chris', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boat-rides-boston-harbor-captain-chris-2026-10-02', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boat-rides-boston-harbor-captain-chris-2026-10-02', FALSE, '2026-10-02T12:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-02T13:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Fan Pier', 'Fan Pier, Boston, MA', 'Boston', 'Meetup', 'Boston AI Week', '[]'::jsonb, 'Boat Rides of Boston Harbor with Captain Chris', 'Join Captain Chris for a boat ride on Boston Harbor, offered every day of Boston AI Week''s core week (September 24 – October 2). Very limited availability. RSVP to be considered, we''ll work with Captain Chris on approvals. Due to expected demand, not all RSVPs will be honored, but we''ll do our best.

Time: TBA. This is a request, not a confirmed spot — organizers will follow up if your RSVP is approved.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boat-rides-boston-harbor-captain-chris-2026-10-02']::text[], now()),
  ('the-human-role-in-ai-driven-businesses-c-dean-metropoulos-institute-for-technolo@aiweek.boston', 'The Human Role in AI-Driven Businesses, C. Dean Metropoulos Institute for Technology and Entrepreneurship', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/the-human-role-in-ai-driven-businesses-c-dean-metropoulos-institute-for-technolo', 'https://cvent.me/MAqDbL', TRUE, '2026-10-02T16:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-02T18:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Babson Boston', '100 High Street, Floor 12, Boston, MA 02110', 'Boston', 'Panel', 'Babson College', '[{"name":"Paul Cheek","bio":"Senior Advisor for Entrepreneurship & AI at the C. Dean Metropoulos Institute for Technology and Entrepreneurship at Babson''s Arthur M. Blank School for Entrepreneurial Leadership, and author of No One Works Here."},{"name":"Tom Davenport","bio":"Faculty Director of the C. Dean Metropoulos Institute for Technology and Entrepreneurship and Babson College Distinguished Professor of IT and Management, author of The New Science of Customer Relationships."}]'::jsonb, 'The Human Role in AI-Driven Businesses, C. Dean Metropoulos Institute for Technology and Entrepreneurship', 'As AI transforms the workplace, what remains uniquely human and what doesn''t?

Join Babson College thought leader Tom Davenport and Arthur M. Blank School for Entrepreneurial Leadership senior advisor Paul Cheek for a timely conversation on the future of business in an AI-driven world. While both see AI fundamentally reshaping organizations, they offer distinct perspectives on what comes next, from AI-enabled customer relationships to enterprises where traditional work may look radically different. Hosted by the C. Dean Metropoulos Institute for Technology and Entrepreneurship at the Arthur M. Blank School for Entrepreneurial Leadership and moderated by the Institute''s Executive Director, the program will include an audience Q&A, networking reception, and book signing.

Attendees will receive complimentary copies of Tom Davenport''s The New Science of Customer Relationships and Paul Cheek''s No One Works Here, while supplies last.

Speakers

Paul Cheek, Senior Advisor, Entrepreneurship & AI, C. Dean Metropoulos Institute for Technology and Entrepreneurship at the Arthur M. Blank School for Entrepreneurial Leadership

Tom Davenport, Faculty Director, C. Dean Metropoulos Institute for Technology and Entrepreneurship at the Arthur M. Blank School for Entrepreneurial Leadership,

Babson College Distinguished Professor of IT and Management

Executive Director, C. Dean Metropoulos Institute for Technology and Entrepreneurship at the Arthur M. Blank School for Entrepreneurial Leadership', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/the-human-role-in-ai-driven-businesses-c-dean-metropoulos-institute-for-technolo']::text[], now()),
  ('boston-ai-week-closing-party@aiweek.boston', 'Core Week Closing Party', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boston-ai-week-closing-party', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boston-ai-week-closing-party', FALSE, '2026-10-02T17:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-02T20:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Island Creek Oyster Raw Bar', '99 Autumn Lane, Boston, MA 02210', 'Boston', 'Community', 'Boston AI Week', '[]'::jsonb, 'Core Week Closing Party', 'Closing celebration for Boston AI Week 2026 at Island Creek Oyster Raw Bar in the Seaport.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boston-ai-week-closing-party']::text[], now()),
  ('mit-futurefest-2026-day-4@aiweek.boston', 'MIT Future Fest — Day 4: Human Futures Unfolding', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/mit-futurefest-2026-day-4', 'https://mitfuturefest.org/festival-passes', TRUE, '2026-10-03T10:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-03T21:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'MIT Campus', '77 Massachusetts Avenue, Cambridge, MA 02139', 'Cambridge', 'Community', 'MIT Future Fest (MIT Museum, MIT Technology Review & MIT Morningside Academy for Design)', '[]'::jsonb, 'MIT Future Fest — Day 4: Human Futures Unfolding', 'Part of MIT Future Fest, the inaugural five-day festival where MIT opens its doors to the world — more than 50 events across campus spanning immersive exhibitions, open laboratories, public talks, and unexpected performances, presented by the MIT Museum, MIT Technology Review, and the MIT Morningside Academy for Design. One festival pass covers all five days. Session times vary; the full program launches August 17 at mitfuturefest.org/program.

The penultimate day opens with the landmark public debut of the MET Warehouse, MIT''s new home for design and architecture. Across campus the day moves through urgent, personal questions — the future of learning, of work, of space, of what it means to be human in an age of AI — with students, founders, and makers filling the festival with invention before an evening of live music and a ground-breaking theatrical premiere.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/mit-futurefest-2026-day-4']::text[], now()),
  ('venture-signal-founder-investor-exchange-how-investors-evaluate-ai-native-and-ai@aiweek.boston', '​Venture Signal Founder & Investor Exchange: ​How Investors Evaluate AI-Native and AI-Enabled Startups', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/venture-signal-founder-investor-exchange-how-investors-evaluate-ai-native-and-ai', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/venture-signal-founder-investor-exchange-how-investors-evaluate-ai-native-and-ai', FALSE, '2026-10-03T14:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-03T16:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Venue: Private venue in Brighton, Boston', NULL, 'Boston', 'Community', 'TopContenders Network', '[]'::jsonb, '​Venture Signal Founder & Investor Exchange: ​How Investors Evaluate AI-Native and AI-Enabled Startups', 'Venture Signal Founder & Investor Exchange

How Investors Evaluate AI-Native and AI-Enabled Startups

Conversations, company building, and meaningful connections.

Presented by TopContenders Network as part of Boston AI Week 2026

📅 Saturday, October 3, 2026

⏰ 2:00–4:00 PM ET

📍 Address provided to registered participants

🚗 Complimentary parking available

About the Event

Join TopContenders Network for a focused, conversational gathering connecting AI founders, investors, scientists, executives, and innovation professionals.

This edition of the Venture Signal Founder & Investor Exchange will focus on AI-native companies and startups using AI to transform established industries, including healthcare, biotech, enterprise technology, robotics, and other emerging fields.

Together, we will explore what makes an AI company genuinely differentiated and investable—and how investors distinguish durable businesses from companies simply adding AI to an existing story.

The program is expected to include: Structured introductions and purposeful relationship-building

A practical discussion of how investors evaluate AI startups

A case study based on a company backed by VU Venture Partners

A featured presentation from an early-stage AI-native or AI-enabled company

Constructive questions, investor perspectives, and group discussion

Topics may include market potential, proprietary data, defensibility, technical differentiation, customer value, team capabilities, AI infrastructure costs, financing readiness, and the milestones that can move a company from initial interest toward investment.

Additional program details, guest contributors, and a potential co-host will be announced as plans are finalized.

Apply to Present

One early-stage AI-native or AI-enabled company will be selected to present its work and receive constructive questions and feedback from the group.

Apply for the startup presentation opportunity

The selected founder will be contacted before the event. Participation does not imply endorsement or investment consideration.

Who Should Attend?

Founders building AI-native or AI-enabled companies

Current and aspiring investors interested in AI

Scientists, engineers, and industry executives

Researchers and professionals exploring the commercialization of AI

Members of Boston’s startup and innovation community

About TopContenders Network

TopContenders Network brings together entrepreneurs, investors, industry experts, and innovation leaders to Meet, Connect, Learn, and Collaborate.

Venture Signal is a TopContenders Network initiative creating focused conversations, practical learning, and meaningful connections across the startup and investment ecosystem.

Registration

Attendance is complimentary, but advance registration is required.

Please come prepared to briefly introduce yourself and share what you are working on, what you hope to find, or how you may be able to help others.

Disclosures

This event is organized for educational and networking purposes. The views expressed by individual participants are their own and do not necessarily represent VU Venture Partners or any other affiliated organization.

Nothing presented constitutes an offer to sell or a solicitation to purchase securities, or investment, legal, tax, or financial advice.

Photos or brief recordings may be taken for event-recap and promotional purposes. Please notify the organizer if you prefer not to be included.

Presented by TopContenders Network

A Boston AI Week 2026 Program', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/venture-signal-founder-investor-exchange-how-investors-evaluate-ai-native-and-ai']::text[], now()),
  ('mit-futurefest-2026-day-5@aiweek.boston', 'MIT Future Fest — Day 5: Futures Made Together', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/mit-futurefest-2026-day-5', 'https://mitfuturefest.org/festival-passes', TRUE, '2026-10-04T10:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-04T21:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'MIT Campus', '77 Massachusetts Avenue, Cambridge, MA 02139', 'Cambridge', 'Community', 'MIT Future Fest (MIT Museum, MIT Technology Review & MIT Morningside Academy for Design)', '[]'::jsonb, 'MIT Future Fest — Day 5: Futures Made Together', 'Part of MIT Future Fest, the inaugural five-day festival where MIT opens its doors to the world — more than 50 events across campus spanning immersive exhibitions, open laboratories, public talks, and unexpected performances, presented by the MIT Museum, MIT Technology Review, and the MIT Morningside Academy for Design. One festival pass covers all five days. Session times vary; the full program launches August 17 at mitfuturefest.org/program.

The festival closes as the Cambridge Science Carnival returns to the Kendall/MIT Open Space, welcoming visitors of all ages to more than 100 hands-on STEAM experiences — from the beloved Robot Petting Zoo to an Oceans zone featuring leading marine science institutions. A celebration of curiosity and creativity, and the perfect finale to MIT Future Fest''s inaugural edition.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/mit-futurefest-2026-day-5']::text[], now()),
  ('ai-in-action-boston-2026@aiweek.boston', 'AI in Action', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-in-action-boston-2026', 'https://boston.theaia.ai/', TRUE, '2026-10-06T09:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-06T18:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Harvard Club of Boston', '374 Commonwealth Ave, Boston, MA', 'Boston', 'Conference', 'AI in Action', '[]'::jsonb, 'AI in Action', 'AI isn''t just about innovation, it''s about execution. Join founders, startups, executives, investors, innovators and AI leaders at AI in Action on October 6 at the Harvard Club of Boston. A full day of practical AI strategies, real-world case studies, meaningful networking and actionable insights you can take back to your organization. Whether you''re scaling a startup, leading enterprise AI initiatives or exploring what''s next, this event is designed to help you turn AI into measurable business results. Exclusive registration savings — use code: AIA15%aiweek. Startups can apply for 50% off. Secure your spot today: boston.theaia.ai', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-in-action-boston-2026']::text[], now()),
  ('the-150-million-secret-free-money-free-fellows-and-free-time-back@aiweek.boston', 'The $150 Million Secret: Free Money, Free Fellows, and Free Time Back', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/the-150-million-secret-free-money-free-fellows-and-free-time-back', 'https://us06web.zoom.us/meeting/register/bHP033WKTCCAuJEovzn5jw', TRUE, '2026-10-06T12:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-06T13:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Dedham, MA (exact address shared with registrants once the venue is confirmed)', NULL, 'Virtual', 'Talk', 'Northeast Professionals in Artificial Intelligence', '[{"name":"Dr. Beth Griffith","bio":"President of NEPAI (Northeast Professionals in Artificial Intelligence) and Executive Director of Gigs Uplift Mass; has trained more than 587 business owners and workers, mentored over 1,400 people, and helped secure over $2 million in forgivable funding for gig workers."}]'::jsonb, 'The $150 Million Secret: Free Money, Free Fellows, and Free Time Back', 'Your nonprofit lost money this month. Not because you overspent, but because money set aside for organizations like yours went unclaimed, again.

Somewhere out there is a grant with your name on it, quietly funding somebody else. A funder who never needs to be repaid said yes to an organization half your size because they knew the program existed and you did not. A fellow whose salary is covered by someone else is building someone else''s program right now. And your best people, the ones who came for the mission, spent tonight retyping receipts into a spreadsheet.

You know the feeling. It is 11 PM and you are the executive director, the development office, the bookkeeper, the receptionist, and the IT department, all at once. The report is due Friday. The thank-you notes are three weeks behind. The board keeps asking about your AI strategy, and you have not had time to eat lunch, never mind build one.

Here is the part that should make you angry: the help you need already exists, it is free, and nobody handed you the map.

For one hour, Dr. Beth Griffith hands you the map. The $150 million secret comes apart in three pieces:

FREE MONEY. The grants, forgivable funding, and giving programs built for organizations exactly like yours, including the funders who say yes to first-time applicants, and how to position your story so a reviewer sees a fundable organization instead of a risky bet.

FREE FELLOWS. The fellowship, service corps, and internship pipelines that place capable, paid people inside your organization while someone else covers the check. Who qualifies as a host site, what the applications really require, and the deadlines that decide whether you get a fellow this year or watch another one build somebody else''s program.

FREE TIME BACK. The Digital Helpers a nonprofit can set up without writing a single line of code: one that drafts your donor thank-you notes, one that answers the phone while your one staffer is at lunch, one that chases the paperwork so your program director can go back to the program. People first. AI second. Impact always.

You walk out with a claim map of every resource named in the session, the deadlines that matter over the next 12 months, and the first three steps to take this quarter. Come with your board chair and you will leave with your next board meeting agenda already written.

Who this is for: executive directors, founders, board members, program managers, development staff, and volunteers at small and midsize nonprofits, especially community-based organizations doing big work on small budgets. No AI experience needed. Total beginners are the entire point of the room.

Your presenter has the receipts: Dr. Beth Griffith, AIC, BAP, GIP, HIC, LEED, is President of NEPAI (Northeast Professionals in Artificial Intelligence), whose mission is simple: AI is for everyone. She serves as Executive Director of Gigs Uplift Mass, has trained more than 587 business owners and workers, mentored over 1,400 people, facilitated nearly 3,600 tax returns generating $7 million in refunds for low- and middle-income individuals, and helped secure over $2 million in forgivable funding for gig workers. She has spent her career finding the free money. This is the hour she shows you where it lives.

Hybrid and free: join on Zoom from anywhere, or be in the room at a Dedham, Massachusetts location shared with registrants once confirmed. Nothing is sold. Bring your biggest question and your budget headache.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/the-150-million-secret-free-money-free-fellows-and-free-time-back']::text[], now()),
  ('beyond-the-chatbot-a-framework-for-finding-where-ai-pays-off@aiweek.boston', 'Beyond the Chatbot: A Framework for Finding Where AI Pays Off', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/beyond-the-chatbot-a-framework-for-finding-where-ai-pays-off', 'https://elasticpath.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_4Ndo5sbhSN-lJi2ULDx8iw', TRUE, '2026-10-06T13:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-06T14:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, NULL, NULL, 'Virtual', 'Talk', 'Elastic Path', '[{"name":"Bryan House","bio":"CEO at Elastic Path; previously Chief Commercial Officer at Neural Magic, Acquia founding team member, and MBA from Harvard Business School."}]'::jsonb, 'Beyond the Chatbot: A Framework for Finding Where AI Pays Off', 'Ask most business leaders where AI is working inside their organization and they''ll point to a chatbot. Walmart, Amazon, and Albertsons have proven that model works — but customer-facing chat has also become the most crowded, commoditized category in AI, and most companies default to it not because it''s the highest-return option, but because it''s the most obvious one.

This session argues the opposite of what gets funded first: the AI use cases with the biggest financial and operational return are usually the ones that you need to dig a layer deeper to find. Using Elastic Path’s own AI-based software implementation as a working example, the company''s CEO Bryan House walks through how to choose the highest impact AI application for your business — and introduces a framework attendees can use immediately to score their own use cases and build a prioritized list of where to invest next.

This session is built for the people making AI investment decisions — digital strategy leads, product owners, and operators — who want a repeatable method for separating flashy from valuable. Come with two or three AI use cases you''re weighing; you''ll leave having scored them and knowing which one to build first.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/beyond-the-chatbot-a-framework-for-finding-where-ai-pays-off']::text[], now()),
  ('from-lean-to-the-intelligent-enterprise-how-ai-is-reshaping-business-transformat@aiweek.boston', 'From Lean to the Intelligent Enterprise; How AI is reshaping business transformation, and enterprise value creation', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/from-lean-to-the-intelligent-enterprise-how-ai-is-reshaping-business-transformat', 'https://luma.com/4rk7eykz', TRUE, '2026-10-07T08:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-07T12:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'EF Education First', 'Two Education Cir, Cambridge, MA 02141', 'Cambridge', 'Panel', 'Monomoy Advisors', '[]'::jsonb, 'From Lean to the Intelligent Enterprise; How AI is reshaping business transformation, and enterprise value creation', 'Roundtable discussion, with Transformation Leaders, COOs, PE Operating Partners, and Operational Excellence leaders to share from experience and lessons learned.

Discussions to explore include:

Scaling AI enabled transformation beyond pilots into real enterprise impact

Proving process change— and compounding — the ROI of transformation

Turning operational gains into lasting enterprise value', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/from-lean-to-the-intelligent-enterprise-how-ai-is-reshaping-business-transformat']::text[], now()),
  ('ai-leadership-workshop-for-hr-learning-executives@aiweek.boston', 'AI Leadership Workshop for HR & Learning Executives', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-leadership-workshop-for-hr-learning-executives', 'https://www.kendallai.org/261007-hr-learning-leader-ai-leadership-workshop/', TRUE, '2026-10-07T10:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-07T15:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Cambridge Innovation Center, Mosaic Room', '245 Main St, Cambridge MA', 'Cambridge', 'Workshop', 'The Kendall Project', '[{"name":"Kevin Gulley","bio":"Co-Founder and Managing Partner of The Kendall Project; technology entrepreneur and product development leader across data, AI, fintech, digital media, and gaming."},{"name":"Brendan McSheffrey","bio":"Managing Partner of The Kendall Project and serial entrepreneur; background spans SaaS, healthcare, defense, financial services, IoT, and privacy technologies."}]'::jsonb, 'AI Leadership Workshop for HR & Learning Executives', 'Join fellow HR and Learning & Development leaders for a hands-on, half-day workshop designed to help organizations move beyond AI awareness and experimentation toward building lasting AI capability across the workforce.

Generative AI is not another digital transformation initiative. It is changing how knowledge work gets done, how decisions are made, and how employees interact with the processes, policies, and institutional knowledge that define an organization. While many companies have invested in AI awareness sessions, tool training, and experimentation, far fewer have built the operating foundation required to make AI useful, trustworthy, and scalable across the organization.

That creates an important opportunity for HR and L&D leaders.

As AI becomes embedded in everyday work, HR and L&D have the potential to play a much larger role than simply delivering AI training. They can help build the organizational capability that allows people to use AI effectively, identify where AI can create meaningful value, and ensure AI has the context it needs to perform accurately.

During this interactive workshop, The Kendall Project will introduce participants to the Kendall Framework, a structured approach for moving organizations from AI awareness to AI capability.

Participants will work through real challenges from their own organizations while exploring:

- Why AI workforce programs often plateau after initial training and experimentation

- What makes AI transformation different from traditional change management

- How HR and L&D can become strategic enablers of enterprise AI performance

- How to identify and prioritize high-value AI opportunities across HR and the broader workforce

- Why organizational context, including roles, policies, processes, workflows, and institutional knowledge, is critical to AI performance

- How to build AI capability inside the organization rather than creating long-term dependency on outside vendors or individual AI experts

- What leadership alignment is required to move from experimentation to execution

Participants will also apply a problem-first approach to identify potential AI opportunities across areas such as talent acquisition, onboarding, learning design, performance management, workforce planning, and other knowledge-intensive workflows.

A major focus of the workshop will be the Context Gap: the gap between what AI knows generically and what it needs to know about your organization to perform effectively. Participants will explore how the knowledge employees use every day can be captured, structured, governed, and made usable by AI.

This is not a lecture or a traditional AI training session. Every session is designed to be interactive. Participants will work through exercises, discuss what is actually happening inside their organizations, compare experiences with other HR and L&D leaders, and begin building an actionable path forward.

Participants will leave with:

- A clearer understanding of what leadership alignment around AI actually requires

- A pathway for building evergreen AI skills across the workforce

- A practical understanding of the Context Gap and the role HR and L&D can play in closing it

- A structured method for identifying and prioritizing AI opportunities

- A practical leadership framework for moving from AI awareness to execution

- An immediate next-steps plan they can take back to their organization

Who should attend:

- Chief Human Resources Officers and Chief People Officers

- VPs and Directors of Learning & Development

- Heads of Talent Development and Organizational Effectiveness

- HR transformation leaders responsible for workforce AI readiness

- Senior HR business partners advising leadership on AI strategy

The workshop is designed for leaders who recognize that the next stage of enterprise AI will require more than giving employees access to new tools. Organizations need to build the skills, context, processes, and ownership required to make AI a durable organizational capability.

Date: Wednesday, October 7, 2026

Time: 10:00 AM – 3:00 PM ET

Location: Cambridge Innovation Center, 245 Main Street, Cambridge, MA

Format: In-person, facilitated workshop

Lunch: Included

Attendance is intentionally limited to 50 participants to keep the workshop highly interactive and give attendees the opportunity to work through real organizational challenges alongside fellow HR and L&D leaders.

Fewer awareness sessions. More AI capability your organization can actually use.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-leadership-workshop-for-hr-learning-executives']::text[], now()),
  ('ai-disability-and-the-future-of-work-2026@aiweek.boston', 'Artificial Intelligence, Disability, and the Future of Work', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-disability-and-the-future-of-work-2026', 'https://www.mass.gov/news/2026-national-disability-employment-awareness-month-celebration', TRUE, '2026-10-07T14:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-07T15:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Massachusetts State House, Room 428', '24 Beacon Street, Boston, MA 02133', 'Boston', 'Panel', 'Massachusetts Permanent Commission on the Status of Persons with Disabilities', '[]'::jsonb, 'Artificial Intelligence, Disability, and the Future of Work', 'The Massachusetts Permanent Commission on the Status of Persons with Disabilities hosts its first panel discussion on artificial intelligence, examining how AI is reshaping employment and what that means for disabled workers across the Commonwealth.

The panel is part of the Commission''s 2026 National Disability Employment Awareness Month celebration at the Massachusetts State House.

ASL and CART services have been requested for this session.

Registration and the full celebration program are on the Commission''s Mass.gov page.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-disability-and-the-future-of-work-2026']::text[], now()),
  ('gtm-under-pressure-the-ai-mystery-basket-challenge@aiweek.boston', 'GTM Under Pressure: The AI Mystery Basket Challenge', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/gtm-under-pressure-the-ai-mystery-basket-challenge', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/gtm-under-pressure-the-ai-mystery-basket-challenge', FALSE, '2026-10-08T10:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-08T12:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Fenway Community Center', '1282 Boylston Street (entrance on Jersey St.), Boston, MA 02215', 'Boston', 'Workshop', 'AI Advisor Guy + KAWAF', '[{"name":"Kham Inthirath","bio":"AI Advisor Guy."},{"name":"Ace","bio":"KAWAF."}]'::jsonb, 'GTM Under Pressure: The AI Mystery Basket Challenge', 'Most AI events show you tools. This one puts you to work.

AI has made it easier than ever to research markets, understand buyers, create content, personalize outreach, automate follow-up, and build increasingly agentic workflows. The harder question is:

How do you turn all of those capabilities into a go-to-market motion that actually works?

GTM Under Pressure: The AI Mystery Basket Challenge is a hands-on, competitive workshop designed for GTM, sales, marketing, growth, revenue, and RevOps leaders who want to move beyond AI experimentation and learn how to apply AI strategically across the modern GTM lifecycle.

We’ll start with a fast-paced framework covering the Six Jobs of Modern GTM, from market intelligence and ICP development to positioning, activation, conversion, and measurement. We’ll also walk through frontier GTM workflows that show how AI can move beyond simple prompting into connected workflows and increasingly agentic systems, while keeping human judgment, governance, and responsible AI in the loop.

Then the mystery basket opens.

Participants will be placed into intentionally mixed teams and given the same unfamiliar product and market, along with a unique business constraint. Each team will have 50 minutes to research the opportunity, form an ICP hypothesis, develop positioning, choose a GTM motion, identify where AI creates meaningful leverage, and prepare a 90-second pitch.

Use ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Perplexity, or whatever AI tools you already know. We’ll also provide an optional GTM Sous Chef, an AI advisor built specifically to help teams pressure-test their thinking during the challenge.

Think:

MBA capstone meets GTM strategy lab meets a mystery-basket cooking challenge.

The session ends with rapid-fire team pitches, live judging, and a winner.

You’ll leave with a practical modern GTM framework, experience using AI to solve an unfamiliar business problem under pressure, a repeatable GTM Challenge Canvas, AI workflows and prompts you can take back to your team, and access to a GTM Advisor you can use after the event.

This is not another sit-and-listen AI session. Come ready to think, build, experiment, compete, and see what modern GTM looks like when AI becomes part of the operating system.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/gtm-under-pressure-the-ai-mystery-basket-challenge']::text[], now()),
  ('cambridge-ai-civil-rights-proclamation@aiweek.boston', 'Cambridge''s AI Civil Rights Proclamation & Hope Day Commemoration', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/cambridge-ai-civil-rights-proclamation', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/cambridge-ai-civil-rights-proclamation', FALSE, '2026-10-08T18:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-08T20:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Sullivan Chamber, Cambridge City Hall', '795 Massachusetts Ave, Cambridge, MA', 'Cambridge', 'Community', 'City of Cambridge with The Loop Lab', '[]'::jsonb, 'Cambridge''s AI Civil Rights Proclamation & Hope Day Commemoration', 'On Thursday, October 8, the City of Cambridge puts its position on the record: a formal proclamation supporting the Artificial Intelligence Civil Rights Act (H.R. 6356 / S. 3308), introduced by Senator Edward J. Markey and Representative Yvette Clarke, and commemorating Hope Day, Cambridge''s standing commitment that the digital divide is a civil rights issue.

The bill asks that the same fairness, transparency, and accountability required of human decision-makers apply to the algorithms increasingly making decisions about housing, hiring, lending, and public services. Massachusetts is moving at the state level as well: Representative Andy Vargas, chair of the Massachusetts Black and Latino Legislative Caucus, is reintroducing companion legislation reflecting the same principles.

Hope Day grew out of the work of Rev. Christopher Hope, founder of The Loop Lab and The Hope Group, who has spent nine years building registered apprenticeship pathways for opportunity youth in creative technology and artificial intelligence, and who collaborated with Senator Markey''s office on the legislation. Its animating principle is the Digital Beloved Community: the belief that technology''s advance is only worth celebrating if it draws every neighborhood closer to dignity and opportunity.

"This is a new frontier of civil rights, and it''s moving faster than most laws can keep up," Rev. Hope has said. "Fairness, transparency, and accountability aren''t optional when algorithms are making decisions about people''s lives."

A certified copy of the proclamation will be transmitted to Senator Markey, Senator Warren, Representative Pressley, and Representative Clark, formally entering Cambridge''s position into the congressional record. The City has also committed to applying principles of algorithmic fairness, transparency, and accountability in its own use of AI, a commitment residents, institutions, and community organizations will be invited to help shape.

Hope Day itself arrives October 28, when Boston AI Week closes with Celebrate Hope at Cambridge City Hall. All are welcome on October 8.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/cambridge-ai-civil-rights-proclamation']::text[], now()),
  ('boston-openclaw-meetup@aiweek.boston', 'Boston OpenClaw meetup', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boston-openclaw-meetup', 'https://luma.com/jdun7rei', TRUE, '2026-10-08T18:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-08T20:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Microsoft NERD', '1 Memorial Drive, Cambridge, MA, 02142', 'Cambridge', 'Community', 'Boston OpenClaw', '[]'::jsonb, 'Boston OpenClaw meetup', 'Inviting all personal agent enthusiasts to join a day of sharing with the Boston OpenClaw community. No matter what agent you use, whether it''s an OpenClaw, a Hermes agent, a Grok bot, or anything else, everyone is welcome. We invite you all to come and share your personal agent use cases, setup, and experiences.

No selling, no pitches of startups, just pure sharing with the community so that we all can learn from each other.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boston-openclaw-meetup']::text[], now()),
  ('stop-prompting-start-delegating-build-your-first-ai-agent-live-in-90-minutes@aiweek.boston', 'Stop Prompting, Start Delegating: Build Your First AI Agent Live in 90 Minutes', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/stop-prompting-start-delegating-build-your-first-ai-agent-live-in-90-minutes', 'https://buildmyagent.imarahealthai.com/workshop', TRUE, '2026-10-10T10:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-10T11:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, NULL, NULL, 'Virtual', 'Workshop', 'Imara Health AI', '[{"name":"Jennifer Ngure","bio":"Founder and CEO of Imara Health AI Consulting LLC; nurse turned AI strategist with twenty years implementing technology across 270+ organizations; author of the Amazon #1 New Release S.E.C.U.R.E. Your Workforce."}]'::jsonb, 'Stop Prompting, Start Delegating: Build Your First AI Agent Live in 90 Minutes', 'Most people use AI like an intern they re-onboard every single morning. Explain the job, get the output, and tomorrow explain it again from scratch. That is why it is not saving you any time.

I do not prompt. I delegate. I run my consulting business with a named team of AI agents, each with a defined role and its own standing instructions. One drafts content. One checks quality before anything ships. One reads my numbers back to me. One routes the work between the others. I will show you the actual roster on screen.

Then we build yours. Live, together, in this session.

Come with your laptop open and one task in mind: the thing you repeat most and enjoy least. By the end of 90 minutes you will have an agent set up, tested, and doing that task. Not a plan to build one. A built one.

We cover how to choose the right first agent, how to write instructions that actually hold up, and how to fix the two things that break for almost everyone. You will leave with the AI Agent Starter Template, the same one page build sheet I use, plus a map of the next agents to add and what changes once you have five.

What to bring: a laptop and a free Claude or ChatGPT account. No coding. No technical background. If you can write an email, you can do this.

Massachusetts employers: the Commonwealth''s Workforce Training Fund Express Program covers approved AI training for eligible companies at no cost to you. I hold five approved courses, including Building AI Assistants for Small Business. We will cover how to check whether your organization qualifies.

Hosted by Jennifer Ngure, MSHI, PMP, RN, NI-BC. Founder and CEO, Imara Health AI Consulting LLC. Nurse turned AI strategist, twenty years implementing technology across 270+ organizations, author of the Amazon #1 New Release S.E.C.U.R.E. Your Workforce. Human First. AI Second. Always.™', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/stop-prompting-start-delegating-build-your-first-ai-agent-live-in-90-minutes']::text[], now()),
  ('ai-in-finance-lab-boston@aiweek.boston', 'AI in Finance Lab: Boston', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-in-finance-lab-boston', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-in-finance-lab-boston', FALSE, '2026-10-13T12:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-13T18:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Deloitte Boston', '115 Federal Street, Boston, MA 02110', 'Boston', 'Workshop', 'Deloitte', '[]'::jsonb, 'AI in Finance Lab: Boston', 'The AI in Finance Lab is a practitioner-led, hands-on workshop designed to help finance and accounting professionals move beyond AI hype and into real-world application — built specifically for Accounting and FP&A teams.

This is a "Show + Play" Workshop You won''t just watch demos. You''ll actively work through real finance use cases using AI tools during the session. Come ready to experiment, test workflows, and get your hands dirty. Curiosity is the starting point — not prior expertise.

This workshop is for: CFOs / CAOs · VPs of Finance · Controllers · Directors of FP&A, Finance Transformation & Revenue — and members of their teams at enterprise and high-growth tech companies doing the actual work.

This is a practitioner-only event. Vendors and consultants are not eligible to attend.

During the lab you will:

- Learn how AI is already being used across the finance function

- See live demos of real finance workflows across frontier models

- Work through guided, hands-on exercises using AI tools in a lab format

- Close with demos from next-gen AI-native finance software companies

You experiment, understand the mechanics, then decide where software creates leverage. Hands-on experience makes you a better operator and a better buyer.

Agenda: 12:00-1:00pm: Lunch & registration (optional)

1:00-5:30pm: Programming, including interactive demos

5:30-6:30pm: Networking happy hour

What to bring:

- A laptop (required)

- Access to your work email

- Curiosity and willingness to experiment — no prior AI expertise required

We will be running live exercises throughout the session. If you don''t bring a laptop, you will miss a meaningful portion of the experience. If your company restricts access to certain tools, bring a personal email as a backup.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-in-finance-lab-boston']::text[], now()),
  ('agricultural-ai-two-more-kilograms-per-ton@aiweek.boston', 'Agricultural AI: Two More Kilograms Per Ton', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/agricultural-ai-two-more-kilograms-per-ton', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/agricultural-ai-two-more-kilograms-per-ton', FALSE, '2026-10-14T10:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-14T11:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Findability Sciences Office at Workbar Burlington', '100 Summit Dr, Ste 917, Burlington, MA 01880', 'Burlington', 'Talk', 'Findability Sciences', '[{"name":"Sagar Mahurkar","bio":"Vice President at Findability Sciences; TEDx speaker and IEEE award-winning researcher holding 5 US patents, with more than 60 AI projects."}]'::jsonb, 'Agricultural AI: Two More Kilograms Per Ton', 'In the 2025 Pantaleon harvest, validated recovery moved from 81 to 83 percent, roughly two additional kilograms of sugar per ton, with record-low lost time.

The interesting part is not the result but the sequence that produced it. Before any model was built, the mill agreed a reference for its own potential, measured the distance to it, and put its Finance team in charge of the number. This hour walks that sequence: what gets measured at the field-to-harvest and harvest-to-factory handoffs, why purity sets the recovery ceiling, and the five layers that have to hold underneath any of it, being Infrastructure, Collection, Unification, Processing, and Presentation of Data, a framework recognized by IDC, Gartner, and Google. Throughout, the system recommends and the operator acts.

For mill managers, agricultural and factory leadership, and agro-industrial technology teams.

A talk by Sagar Mahurkar, Vice President at Findability Sciences, with live Q&A. Sagar is a TEDx speaker and IEEE award-winning researcher who holds 5 US patents and has worked on more than 60 AI projects himself, drawing on Findability Sciences'' 250+ deployments as a Fortune America''s Most Innovative Company.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/agricultural-ai-two-more-kilograms-per-ton']::text[], now()),
  ('african-women-building-with-ai@aiweek.boston', 'African Women Building With AI', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/african-women-building-with-ai', 'https://africanwomenbuildingwithai.com/', TRUE, '2026-10-17T10:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-17T14:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, NULL, NULL, 'Virtual', 'Conference', 'African Women Building With AI', '[]'::jsonb, 'African Women Building With AI', 'African Women Building With AI is a virtual summit created to help African women across the continent and diaspora use artificial intelligence safely, confidently, and profitably in business, careers, community work, and everyday life.

This four-hour event will bring together women working across AI, business, healthcare, education, technology, leadership, and community development. Through practical talks, demonstrations, guided discussions, and real-world examples, attendees will learn how to move from simply hearing about AI to applying it in ways that address their actual needs and goals.

The summit will explore:

- Practical ways to use AI at work, in business, and in everyday life

- How women can build AI-supported services, products, and income opportunities

- Responsible AI use, including privacy, bias, safety, and human oversight

- Career opportunities and skills needed for an AI-shaped workforce

- How African women can help shape the future of AI—not only consume it

- Ways to build relationships and collaborations across Africa and the diaspora

Attendees will leave with clearer knowledge of what AI can and cannot do, practical ideas they can apply immediately, and connections to women building with AI in different fields and regions.

This event is designed for entrepreneurs, professionals, students, educators, healthcare workers, community leaders, creators, consultants, and women who are curious about AI but may not consider themselves technical.

No coding or prior AI experience is required.

African Women Building With AI supports Boston AI Week’s 2026 theme, “AI in Business and in Life,” by expanding access to practical AI education and bringing African and diaspora perspectives into the wider Massachusetts AI community.

Human First. AI Second. Always.™', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/african-women-building-with-ai']::text[], now()),
  ('ai-education-what-students-and-parents-need-to-know@aiweek.boston', 'AI & Education: What Students and Parents Need to Know', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-education-what-students-and-parents-need-to-know', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-education-what-students-and-parents-need-to-know', FALSE, '2026-10-18T16:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-18T17:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, NULL, NULL, 'Virtual', 'Panel', 'BrainLoop AI Labs', '[]'::jsonb, 'AI & Education: What Students and Parents Need to Know', 'AI is rapidly changing how students learn, how businesses operate, and how future careers will be created. But what does this actually mean for today’s students from high schools to undergrads, and for the parents and educators preparing them for an AI-driven world?

This cross-sector panel brings together school leaders, educators, and business perspectives to explore the questions families need answered now: What does it really mean to be AI-literate? How should schools prepare students for an AI-enabled workforce? What skills will matter as AI increasingly performs technical and knowledge-based tasks? And how can students move beyond simply using AI tools to become critical thinkers, entrepreneurs, problem-solvers, and responsible innovators?

The conversation will connect the dots between education, entrepreneurship, small business, career readiness, and the future of work. Our panelists will explore how entrepreneurial and real-world business thinking can prepare students not only to enter the workforce, but to identify problems, create value, build solutions, and seize new opportunities in an AI-powered economy.

From the classroom, we will examine how AI is changing what and how students should learn, including the evolving role of computer science education. From the world of entrepreneurship and small business, we will explore how AI is lowering barriers to innovation and changing the skills needed to create and grow a business. And from the perspective of career and technical education, we will consider how schools can prepare students for hands-on careers and industries increasingly shaped by AI.

Together, the panel will explore what students, parents, educators, and communities should be doing today to prepare for an AI-driven future, and how we can ensure the next generation is not simply prepared to use AI, but empowered to understand it, question it, build with it, and lead responsibly.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/ai-education-what-students-and-parents-need-to-know']::text[], now()),
  ('boston-startup-demo-competition@aiweek.boston', 'Boston Startup Demo Competition', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boston-startup-demo-competition', 'https://luma.com/epipel-jd9u?tk=HqBRgM', TRUE, '2026-10-21T16:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-21T18:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Epipelagic Ventures', '1 Broadway 15th floor, Cambridge, MA 02142', 'Cambridge', 'Demo Night', 'Epipelagic Ventures', '[]'::jsonb, 'Boston Startup Demo Competition', 'Join Epipelagic Ventures on October 21 to see what founders are building and connect with Boston’s startup community.

Eight companies will demonstrate their products live, answer your questions, and compete for a $3,000 prize. The audience is the judging panel. Your votes decide who wins.

Have a product ready to show? Apply to demo by October 13.

Coming to watch, connect, and vote.

How to Compete

1. Apply to demo: Submit your application by October 13.

2. Get selected: Eight finalists will be confirmed by October 14.

3.Demo live: Each company gets 4 minutes to demonstrate its product, followed by 4 minutes of audience Q&A.

4. Audience votes: After all the demos, the audience votes live.

5. Celebrate the winner: One company takes home $3,000.

Want to Demo Your Product?

We’d love to hear from pre-seed and seed-stage startups across all sectors that have:

-A working product or demo-ready prototype

-At least one customer, pilot user, or waitlist signup

-Availability to demo in person on October 21 in Cambridge, Massachusetts

Applications close October 13.

Join the Audience and Be the Judge

We welcome founders, investors, and operators from all sectors, along with anyone curious about what’s being built in Boston.

Come meet the founders, ask questions, and share your perspective. Every audience member gets a vote. Help choose who takes home $3,000.

Whether you’ve joined us before or this is your first event, we’d love to see you. Bring a friend, catch up with old ones, and support the founders taking the stage.

Register on this page to attend and cast your vote!', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/boston-startup-demo-competition']::text[], now()),
  ('the-missing-layer-ai-governance-as-organizational-architecture@aiweek.boston', 'The Missing Layer: AI Governance as Organizational Architecture', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/the-missing-layer-ai-governance-as-organizational-architecture', 'https://webforms.pipedrive.com/f/63f0h7oGRmQgjvTC3651w7Kumi1Y2l58DSNOg8YiNXk2YFlBGdEaaemq8rbhSQsqvF', TRUE, '2026-10-21T18:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-21T20:30:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Harvard Club of Boston', '374 Commonwealth Ave, Boston, MA 02115', 'Boston', 'Meetup', 'Bee360', '[{"name":"Sonny Claussen","bio":"CEO & Founder of Bee360."}]'::jsonb, 'The Missing Layer: AI Governance as Organizational Architecture', 'Somewhere in the organization, an AI is doing work nobody approved, on a budget nobody set, producing value nobody is measuring.

That is not a hypothetical. It is what technology leaders told us this spring — at the MIT Sloan CIO Symposium, and around a table at this same club. Half described their AI governance as guardrails against misuse; more than a third had none at all. The most telling number was zero: not one said governance was slowing them down. The problem is not too much AI governance. It is the wrong kind.

The reflex is more rules — a policy, an ethics charter, a framework mapped to NIST. But no federal statute forces the question here. And it is not a rules gap: what is missing is an operating model — clear ownership, budget accountability, and decision rights for every AI in use.

Throughout the roundtable discussion, we will work through:

Why the compliance frameworks everyone is buying cannot close this gap alone

The one question that routes every AI to the right oversight: technology, or teammate?

How to anchor accountability for each AI — functionally, budget-wise, and on results — when the request arrives as "I want ten million dollars of tokens"

This is the missing layer, and closing it separates the organizations scaling AI from those stuck running pilots.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/the-missing-layer-ai-governance-as-organizational-architecture']::text[], now()),
  ('authors-innovators-all-day-book-preview-2026@aiweek.boston', 'Authors & Innovators — All-Day Book Preview', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/authors-innovators-all-day-book-preview-2026', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/authors-innovators-all-day-book-preview-2026', FALSE, '2026-10-22T00:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-23T00:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'UMass Amherst — Mount Ida Campus', 'UMass Amherst Mount Ida Campus, Newton, MA', 'Newton', 'Other', 'Authors & Innovators', '[]'::jsonb, 'Authors & Innovators — All-Day Book Preview', 'Authors & Innovators brings an all-day book preview event to the UMass campus in Newton. Spend the day with the authors and ideas shaping business, technology, and innovation — full lineup and registration details to be announced.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/authors-innovators-all-day-book-preview-2026']::text[], now()),
  ('the-state-of-ai-control-in-institutional-finance@aiweek.boston', 'THE STATE OF AI CONTROL IN INSTITUTIONAL FINANCE', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/the-state-of-ai-control-in-institutional-finance', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/the-state-of-ai-control-in-institutional-finance', FALSE, '2026-10-23T11:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-23T12:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Talk', 'Institutional AI', '[{"name":"Rad H. Pasovschi","bio":"Founder and CEO of Institutional AI and author of The State of AI Control in Institutional Finance — 2026 Edition; computer scientist and business executive with more than three decades of experience in institutional finance."}]'::jsonb, 'THE STATE OF AI CONTROL IN INSTITUTIONAL FINANCE', '80 INSTITUTIONS. 8 SECTORS. 1 QUESTION. We reviewed the public disclosures of asset owners, asset managers, asset servicers, banks, wealth managers, retirement providers, private equity, and insurers.

Our premise was straightforward: AI control must extend deep — beyond the models and agents everyone sees, through the data centers, compute, and power on which they depend — and wide across every partner through which AI is deployed, operated, and ultimately relied upon.

To make that measurable, we built the 5×5 Control Matrix™ and applied the same evidence standard across all 80 institutions — 2,000 evaluations through one consistent framework, drawn entirely from the public record.

Across all eight sectors, the public record reveals a remarkably consistent pattern: the implications extend well beyond technology.

—

The State of AI Control in Institutional Finance — 2026 is now available to qualifying institutions.

See where institutional finance stands — and what it means for your institution.

AI is a given. Control is not.™', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/the-state-of-ai-control-in-institutional-finance']::text[], now()),
  ('celebrate-hope-chris-hope-day-2026@aiweek.boston', 'Celebrate Hope: Join Boston AI Week for Chris Hope Day', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/celebrate-hope-chris-hope-day-2026', 'https://aiweek.boston/schedule/celebrate-hope-chris-hope-day-2026', FALSE, '2026-10-28T00:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-29T00:00:00-04:00'::timestamptz, 'Cambridge City Hall', '795 Massachusetts Ave, Cambridge, MA 02139', 'Cambridge', 'Community', 'Boston AI Week', '[]'::jsonb, 'Celebrate Hope: Join Boston AI Week for Chris Hope Day', 'October 28 is officially Chris Hope Day in the City of Cambridge, and Boston AI Week is bringing the 2026 festival to a close by celebrating the person it honors. Chris Hope is a member of the Boston AI Week Board of Directors, known for his work with The Loop Lab and his leadership in AI ethics and governance. His work embodies what this festival is about: technology in service of people, opportunity, and community.

Join the Boston AI Week community at Cambridge City Hall as we gather with city leaders, colleagues, friends, and neighbors to celebrate Chris, honor the spirit of Chris Hope Day, and close out Boston AI Week 2026 together. The Festival of AI ends the way it began: with the community that makes it possible.

Time to be announced. All are welcome.', ARRAY['https://aiweek.boston/schedule/celebrate-hope-chris-hope-day-2026']::text[], now())
